*  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let the unique uncovered unit square be at column \\(f(i)\\), where  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation (bijection).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,f(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nand every row and every column contains exactly one element of \\(U\\).\n\nAll remaining squares must be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles, each tile being a set of unit squares of the form  \n\n\\[\nR=R_{\\text{rows}}\\times R_{\\text{cols}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R_{\\text{rows}}\\) and \\(R_{\\text{cols}}\\) are intervals of consecutive rows and columns, respectively.  \nNo tile may contain a square of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound – a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<f(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid f(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is the set of squares left (resp. right) of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nBoth \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) are (possibly empty) horizontal intervals, hence each is a legal tile of height 1.\n\nPlace a tile covering \\(L_i\\) whenever \\(L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\) and a tile covering \\(R_i\\) whenever \\(R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\).  \nExactly one row has \\(f(i)=1\\) (its left part is empty) and exactly one row has \\(f(i)=n\\) (its right part is empty).  \nConsequently the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-2)}_{\\text{rows with both parts}} \\!\\times\\!2\n\\;+\\;2\n=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nAll squares except those of \\(U\\) are covered, and the construction respects the grid‑line condition.  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum}\\le 2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nSplit the board into the **left region**\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<f(i)\\}\n\\]\n\nand the **right region**\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>f(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(\\mathcal R\\) are disjoint and together contain all squares that must be tiled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1 (left region).  \nAny tiling of \\(\\mathcal L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(i_k\\) with \\(f(i_k)=k+1\\) (since \\(f\\) is a permutation).  \nIn row \\(i_k\\) the left part consists of the cells \\((i_k,1),\\dots ,(i_k,k)\\); the cell \\((i_k,k)\\) is the **rightmost** cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) in that row.\n\nConsider any tile \\(T\\) covering \\((i_k,k)\\).  \nBecause column \\(k+1\\) contains the hole \\((i_k,k+1)\\), \\(T\\) cannot extend to column \\(k+1\\); hence the **rightmost column** of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(k\\).  \nConsequently each tile covering a portion of \\(\\mathcal L\\) possesses a well‑defined rightmost column, and distinct tiles may have the same rightmost column only if they are disjoint in rows.\n\nSince for each \\(k\\) the cell \\((i_k,k)\\) must be covered, there must be at least one tile whose rightmost column equals \\(k\\).  \nThus we need at least one tile for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\), i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) tiles. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2 (right region).  \nAny tiling of \\(\\mathcal R\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(j_k\\) with \\(f(j_k)=k-1\\).  \nIn row \\(j_k\\) the right part consists of the cells \\((j_k,k),\\dots ,(j_k,n)\\); the cell \\((j_k,k)\\) is the **leftmost** cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\) in that row.\n\nIf a tile \\(T\\) covers \\((j_k,k)\\), it cannot extend to column \\(k-1\\) (the hole \\((j_k,k-1)\\) lies there); therefore the **leftmost column** of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(k\\).  \nAs before, each value \\(k\\) forces the existence of a tile whose l