ight region for a row j includes columns > f(j). So if tile's rightmost column = k, it could include a cell (j,b) with b <= k, but b > f(j). So we need f(j) < b <= k. So if there exists a row j such that f(j) < k, then the tile could include a right region cell with column b between f(j)+1 and k. However, the tile's column interval is [c,k] where c is leftmost column of tile. Since tile includes (i_k,k) which is column k, and cannot extend beyond column k. So tile's column interval includes columns up to k. It could include columns less than k as well. So it's possible that tile includes a right region cell (j,b) where b <= k. But is that allowed? Let's examine the shape: In row i_k, uncovered column is k+1. So left region includes columns <= k. Right region includes columns > k+1. So tile covering (i_k,k) cannot include column k+1 (uncovered). So tile's column interval is at most up to k. So tile cannot include any cell in right region of row i_k because those are > k+1. But tile could include right region cells from other rows where uncovered column is less than k (i.e., f(j) < k). For those rows, right region includes columns > f(j). Some of those columns may be <= k. For example, suppose f(j) = 2, then right region includes columns 3..n. So tile's column interval up to k could include column 3 (if k >= 3). So tile could include (j,3) which is in right region. So tile could intersect both L and R across different rows. The lemma 3's argument considered a tile covering a cell in L and a cell in R, but they assumed that the uncovered square in the row of the L cell lies at column a+1 (i.e., right after a). That's true for that row. But they didn't consider that the tile may also cover a cell in R from a different row where the uncovered column is less than a+1, making the tile's column interval not include that row's uncovered square. So the tile could avoid all uncovered squares. So Lemma 3 is false.\n\nThus the lower bound argument fails. However, we need to chec