ths differ, you cannot have a rectangle covering rows i and i+1 with same leftmost column because row i+1 has longer length. For example, you could have rectangle covering rows i+1..n and columns 1..i (i columns). That rectangle would include cell (i,i) which is uncovered (since row i has length i-1, column i is uncovered). So you cannot have rectangle covering rows i+1..n and columns 1..i because it would include uncovered cell (i,i). So you can't combine rows in that way. So indeed you need vertical strips per column. So n-1 rectangles minimal.\n\nThus left region minimal tiles = n-1 for identity permutation. Similarly right region minimal tiles = n-1. So total minimal = 2n-2. So the lower bound of 2n-2 holds at least for identity permutation.\n\nBut could there be a permutation where left region can be tiled with fewer than n-1 rectangles? Let's consider a permutation that is not monotone. The left region shape may be more irregular, perhaps allowing merging of columns across rows. For example, consider permutation f(i) = n+1-i (reverse). Then left region includes cells j < n+1-i, i.e., j <= n-i. So left region is an upper-left triangle (cells above the anti-diagonal). This shape is also a Ferrers diagram (partition (n-1, n-2, ..., 1) but rotated). It also requires n-1 rectangles. So same.\n\nConsider a permutation with some rows having uncovered squares near left side and some near right side. The left region shape may consist of some rows with many left cells and some rows with few left cells. Could we tile left region with fewer than n-1 rectangles by using horizontal strips across rows with similar left region lengths? For example, suppose we have rows where left region length is 0 (uncovered at column 1) and rows where left region length is n-1 (uncovered at column n). The left region includes all cells except those rows with left region length 0 (empty). But we can tile left region across rows with same left region length using horizontal strips. However, each row's left region is a contiguous interval from column 1 up to f(i)-1. So each row's left region is a prefix of columns. So left region is a union of prefixes across rows. This shape is known as a \"staircase\" shape but possibly with gaps. The shape is monotone in the sense that if a cell (i,j) is in left region, then for any row i' with f(i') > f(i) (i.e., longer left region), the cell (i',j) might also be in left region? Not necessarily.\n\nBut perhaps we can tile left region with fewer rectangles by using horizontal strips across multiple rows where left region lengths are the same. For each row i, left region is columns 1..f(i)-1. If we have multiple rows with the same f(i) value, then we can tile those rows' left parts together as a horizontal rectangle covering those rows and columns 1..f(i)-1. However, f is a permutation, so each f(i) is distinct. So each row has a distinct left region length. So no two rows have the same left region length. Therefore we cannot combine rows horizontally because the left region lengths differ. However, we could combine rows with different lengths by using a rectangle that covers columns up to the minimum of the lengths across those rows. For example, rows i and i' with left region lengths a and b (a<b). The rectangle covering rows i,i' and columns 1..a would be a rectangle that fits within both left regions (since both rows have at least columns 1..a). This rectangle would be a valid tile. But then we would need to cover the remaining cells in the longer row (columns a+1..b-1) separately. So we could potentially reduce number of tiles by grouping rows into groups where we take the minimal left region length of each group as a rectangle covering all rows in the group, then handle leftovers. This is reminiscent of a greedy algorithm for partitioning a Ferrers diagram into rectangles: you can partition a Ferrers diagram into at most the number of distinct row lengths (which is n because all lengths are distinct). But we can 