angles. For n=3, lower triangle has cells (2,1) and (3,1),(3,2). That's 3 cells. Could we tile them with 2 rectangles? Yes: one rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1) (vertical strip column1 rows2-3) and another rectangle covering (3,2) (single cell). That's 2 rectangles = n-1 = 2. Could we tile with 1 rectangle? No, because the shape is not a rectangle (it has a missing cell (2,2) which is uncovered). So at least 2 rectangles needed. So n-1 seems minimal for lower triangle. For n=4, lower triangle has 6 cells. Could we tile with 3 rectangles? Possibly: column1 rows2-4 (3 cells), column2 rows3-4 (2 cells), column3 row4 (1 cell). That's 3 rectangles = n-1. Could we tile with 2 rectangles? That would require each rectangle to be a rectangle covering some cells but cannot cover uncovered squares (the diagonal). The shape is like a staircase; any rectangle covering cells from column1 to column2 would also include cell (2,2) which is uncovered. So cannot. So n-1 seems minimal. In general, the lower triangle shape is a Ferrers diagram with partition (n-1, n-2, ..., 1). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Ferrers diagram is equal to the number of rows (or columns) of the diagram? Actually there is a known result: The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram into rectangles is equal to the number of cells on the main diagonal of the diagram (the Durfee square size). But for a staircase shape, the Durfee square size is floor(sqrt(n-1))? Not sure. But we can think: The shape is monotone decreasing rows lengths: row i has length i-1. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition such shape might be the number of rows (n-1). Because each row length is distinct, you cannot combine rows into a rectangle without including a missing cell. So you need at least n-1 rectangles. Indeed, each rectangle can cover at most one cell from each row? Actually a rectangle can cover multiple rows but must have same columns across rows. Since row lengths 