ile has a unique rightmost column, we need at least n-1 distinct tiles. This seems correct.\n\nPotential nuance: Could a tile covering (i_k,k) also cover (i_{k'},k) for some other row i_{k'}? That would be a tile spanning multiple rows, but still its rightmost column is k. The lemma says distinct values of k require distinct tiles. However, could a single tile have rightmost column k and also cover other rows' rightmost cells for other k? Actually each tile can only have one rightmost column (the maximum column of its rectangle). So tile with rightmost column k cannot also have rightmost column k' for k' != k. So each k requires a distinct tile. However, could a tile with rightmost column k cover multiple rows' cells (i_k, k) and maybe also (i_{k'}, k) for some other row? That's okay, but that tile would still count as one tile for that k. So we need at least n-1 tiles, one per k. So lemma holds.\n\nSimilarly for Lemma 2.\n\nLemma 3: No tile can intersect both L and R. The proof: Suppose tile T contains a cell in L and a cell in R. Then there exists some row i where T covers columns a (in L) and b (in R) with a < f(i) < b. Since T's column interval includes both a and b, it must include f(i) as well, which is uncovered. So T cannot intersect both. This is correct.\n\nThus lower bound holds.\n\nUpper bound: The construction uses horizontal strips of height 1 for each row left and right of uncovered square. That yields at most 2 per row, but rows where uncovered square is at leftmost or rightmost have only one tile. So total tiles = 2n - 2. This is a valid tiling.\n\nThus answer is correct, reasoning uses valid methods (permutation model, case analysis, counting argument, construction, proof by contradiction? Actually not needed). All methods are valid.\n\nNow we need to check presentation quality: Are there any justification gaps? The solution is fairly rigorous. However, we need to examine if any steps are missing details or require more justification.\n\nPotential 