rtical and \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and they cover every square except the diagonal ones.  Each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal square).  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  We work with an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares whose rows are numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and whose columns are numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\text{row }i\\text{ has its uncovered square at column }p(i).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, so \\(p\\) is bijective; i.e. \\(p\\) is a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) splits the row into  \n\n* a **left interval** consisting of the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j<p(i)\\); its length is \\(L_i:=p(i)-1\\);\n* a **right interval** consisting of the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(p(i)<j\\le n\\); its length is \\(R_i:=n-p(i)\\).\n\nAll squares of the board belong either to a left interval, to a right interval, or to \\(U\\).  \nConsequently the complement \\(B\\setminus U\\) (the squares that must be tiled) is the disjoint union of the **left region**  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\}\n\\]\n\nand the **right region**  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Shape of the left region  \n\nConsider the rows ordered by increasing value of \\(p(i)\\).  \nLet \\(\\sigma\\) be the permutation that sorts the columns, i.e.  \n\n\\[\np(\\sigma(1))<p(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <p(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\n\nIn this order the lengths of the left intervals become  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\sigma(1)}=0,\\;L_{\\sigma(2)}=1,\\;L_{\\sigma(3)}=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;L_{\\sigma(n)}=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence, after reordering the rows, \\(\\mathcal L\\) is exactly the Young diagram (Ferrers diagram) whose row‑lengths are  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe row of length \\(0\\) contains no squares and can be ignored; the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows have pairwise distinct positive lengths.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for partitioning a Young diagram  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet a Young diagram have rows of distinct positive lengths \\(l_1<l_2<\\dots <l_k\\).  Any partition of the diagram into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least \\(k\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each column of the diagram count how many rows contain that column; this number is the **column height**.  \nBecause the row lengths are distinct, the diagram possesses exactly \\(k\\) distinct column heights (the heights decrease by one at each step of the diagram).  \nConsider any rectangle in a partition.  Its set of rows is a consecutive interval, and its set of columns is a consecutive interval; therefore the rectangle’s column height is constant across its whole interior.  Consequently a rectangle can contain squares of at most one of the distinct column heights.  Since the diagram has \\(k\\) different heights, at least \\(k\\) rectangles are required. ∎\n\nApplying the lemma to \\(\\mathcal L\\) (which has \\(k=n-1\\) distinct positive row lengths) yields\n\n\\[\n\\text{any rectangle partition of }\\mathcal L\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument applied to the right region \\(\\mathcal R\\) (its row lengths are \\(R_i=n-p(i)\\), which after sorting become \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) gives\n\n\\[\n\\text{any rectangle partition of }\\mathcal R\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles (covering the left region).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place a tile  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{V}}=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=c+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) rectangle.  These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover all squares with column index \\(c\\) that lie left of the diagonal.\n\n*Horizontal tiles (covering the right region).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place a tile  \n\n\\[\nT_r^{\\text{H}}=\\{(r,c)\\mid c=r+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-r)\\) rectangle.  These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover all squares with row index \\(r\\) that lie right of the diagonal.\n\nThe two families are disjoint, they together cover every square except the diagonal ones, and each row (resp. column) contains exactly one uncovered square—the diagonal square.  Hence this is a valid tiling using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\b