al tile contains only cells with column > row. Hence no overlap occurs.\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\((n-1)\\) vertical tiles and \\((n-1)\\) horizontal tiles, i.e. \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and satisfies all requirements.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both show that the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a function  \n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\text{row }i\\text{ has its uncovered square at column }p(i).\n\\]  \nSimilarly each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, so \\(p\\) is bijective; i.e. \\(p\\) is a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) splits the row into\n\n* a **left interval** \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\}\\) of length \\(L_i:=p(i)-1\\);\n* a **right interval** \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}\\) of length \\(R_i:=n-p(i)\\).\n\nDefine the two disjoint sets\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nEvery unit square that must be tiled belongs either to \\(\\mathcal L\\) or to \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nA rectangle cannot contain squares from both \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(\\mathcal R\\): if a rectangle covered a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<p(i)\\) and also a cell \\((i,k)\\) with \\(k>p(i)\\) in the same row \\(i\\), then its column interval would contain \\(p(i)\\) and consequently the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\), which is forbidden. Hence each rectangle lies wholly inside \\(\\mathcal L\\) or wholly inside \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nConsequently any tiling of the whole board induces a tiling of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and a tiling of \\(\\mathcal R\\) separately, and the total number of tiles equals the sum of the numbers of tiles used for the two regions.\n\n#### 3.  Shape of the left region  \n\nReorder the rows so that the values \\(p(i)\\) are increasing.  \nLet \\(\\sigma\\) be a permutation with\n\\[\np(\\sigma(1))<p(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <p(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\nIn this order the lengths of the left intervals become\n\\[\nL_{\\sigma(1)}=0,\\;L_{\\sigma(2)}=1,\\;L_{\\sigma(3)}=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;L_{\\sigma(n)}=n-1.\n\\]\nDiscarding the first (empty) row, the set \\(\\mathcal L\\) is exactly the Ferrers diagram\n\\[\nS:=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le c\\le r\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\ni.e. a staircase shape with rows of lengths \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nThe right region \\(\\mathcal R\\) is the transpose of \\(S\\); it has the same combinatorial structure.\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound for a staircase Ferrers diagram  \n\n**Lemma.** In the diagram \\(S=\\{(r,c):1\\le c\\le r\\le n-1\\}\\) any axis‑parallel rectangle contained in \\(S\\) can contain at most one cell of the diagonal\n\\[\nD:=\\{(k,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq S\\) contains two distinct diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) and \\((k,k)\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nBecause \\(R\\) is a rectangle, its row interval contains all rows from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\) and its column interval contains all columns from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\); therefore \\(R\\) also contains the cell \\((i,k)\\).  \nBut \\((i,k)\\notin S\\) because its column exceeds its row (\\(k>i\\)), contradicting \\(R\\subseteq S\\). ∎\n\nThe diagonal \\(D\\) consists of exactly \\(n-1\\) cells. By the lemma each rectangle can cover at most one of them, so any partition of \\(S\\) into rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. The same argument applied to the transposed region \\(\\mathcal R\\) yield