rows of maximal length \\(n-1\\) in \\(R\\)) yields a tiling of \\(R\\) by \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles.  \n\nSince \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, the whole board is tiled by  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{minimum }\\le 2n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are necessary  \n\nWe prove that any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles; the same argument applies to \\(R\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.** *For a Young diagram whose row‑lengths are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), any partition into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.*\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*  \n\n- **Base \\(n=1\\).** The diagram is empty; zero rectangles are needed, and \\(n-1=0\\).  \n\n- **Inductive step.** Let the diagram have \\(n\\ge2\\) rows and let row \\(i\\) be the unique row of length \\(n-1\\).  \n  Column \\(n-1\\) appears only in this row; therefore any rectangle that contains the cell \\((i,n-1)\\) cannot contain any other row, otherwise it would contain a non‑existent cell in that other row.  Consequently the whole row \\(i\\) must be covered by a single horizontal rectangle \\(R_i\\).  \n\n  Removing \\(R_i\\) deletes row \\(i\\) and all its cells.  The remaining diagram has \\(n-1\\) rows, and the lengths of its rows are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  By the induction hypothesis it requires at least \\((n-1)-1=n-2\\) rectangles.  Adding the rectangle \\(R_i\\) gives at least \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\) rectangles for the original diagram. ∎  \n\nApplying Lemma 2 to \\(L\\) yields \\(|\\mathcal{T}_L|\\ge n-1\\), where \\(\\mathcal{T}_L\\) is the set of rectangles covering \\(L\\).  \nThe same reasoning applied to the right region \\(R\\) gives \\(|\\mathcal{T}_R|\\ge n-1\\).  \n\nSince the two families are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}_L|+|\\mathcal{T}_R|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{minimum }\\ge 2n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper and lower bounds coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\displaystyle \\boxed{4048}\\)."
