                "content": ".\n\n**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Model the problem as a biclique partition:**  \n   - The uncovered squares form a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \n   - The set of covered squares is the edge‑set of the bipartite graph \\(K_{n,n}\\) with the perfect matching \\(\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\}\\) removed.  \n   - A rectangular tile corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph (a biclique) \\(R\\times C\\) that does not contain any edge of the removed matching.\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(2n-2\\):**  \n   - For each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering all rows whose hole lies to the right of \\(c\\) (i.e. \\(\\{i:\\pi(i)>c\\}\\times\\{c\\}\\)).  \n   - For each column \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical tile covering all rows whose hole lies to the left of \\(c\\) (i.e. \\(\\{i:\\pi(i)<c\\}\\times\\{c\\}\\)).  \n   - These \\(2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and cover every square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones.\n\n3. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\):**  \n   - Sort the rows so that \\(\\pi(i_1)<\\pi(i_2)<\\dots <\\pi(i_n)\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) consider the “critical” column \\(c_k=\\pi(i_k)-1\\).  \n     *All squares in column \\(c_k\\) belong to rows \\(i_k,i_{k+1},\\dots ,i_n\\) and lie left of every hole in those rows.*  \n     *Any left‑hand tile that covers any of these squares must have its rightmost column exactly \\(c_k\\); otherwise it would contain a hole.*  \n     Hence at least one left‑hand tile is needed for each \\(k\\), giving \\(\\ge n-1\\) left‑hand tiles.  \n   - Symmetrically, for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) the column \\(d_k=\\pi(i_k)+1\\) forces a distinct right‑hand tile, giving another \\(\\ge n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - Consequently any tiling uses at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion:** Both bounds coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row and each column exactly one unit square is left uncovered.  \nHence the uncovered squares form a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\): the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nA rectangular tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists of unit squares.  \nIf a tile covers rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), then it covers precisely the set\n\\[\nR\\times C=\\{(i,j):i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}.\n\\]\nBecause no uncovered square may be covered, we must have\n\\[\n\\pi(i)\\notin C\\qquad\\text{for every }i\\in R,\n\\]\ni.e. \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\pi(R)\\).  \nEquivalently, the bipartite graph formed by the covered squares is the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{n,n}\\) with the perfect matching\n\\[\nM=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nremoved, and each tile corresponds to a biclique (complete bipartite subgraph) of this graph.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Upper bound \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define\n\\[\nR_c^{\\text{L}}=\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)>c\\,\\},\\qquad\nT_c^{\\text{L}}=R_c^{\\text{L}}\\times\\{c\\}.\n\\]\nAll squares of \\(T_c^{\\text{L}}\\) lie left of the uncovered square in each of its rows, so \\(T_c^{\\text{L}}\\) is a legal tile.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct columns.\n\nSimilarly, for each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define\n\\[\nR_c^{\\text{R}}=\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)<c\\,\\},\\qquad\nT_c^{\\text{R}}=R_c^{\\text{R}}\\times\\{c\\}.\n\\]\nEach \\(T_c^{\\text{R}}\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square in its rows, and the \\(n-1\\) tiles are again disjoint.\n\nEvery covered square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies either \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) (it belongs to the left side of its row) or \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) (right side).  \nIf \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) then \\(j\\le n-1\\) and \\(i\\in R_j^{\\text{L}}\\), so \\((i,j)\\in T_j^{\\text{L}}\\).  \nIf \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) then \\(j\\ge2\\) and \\(i\\in R_j^{\\text{R}}\\), so \\((i,j)\\in T_j^{\\text{R}}\\).  \nThus the \\(2n-2\\) tiles \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{L}}\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{T_c^{\\text{R}}\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) cover all squares except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones.  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound \\(2n-2\\)\n\nOrder the rows so that the hole‑columns are increasing:\n\\[\n\\pi(i_1)<\\pi(i_2)<\\dots <\\pi(i_n).\n\\]\nSet \\(a_k=\\pi(i_k)\\) for \\(k=1,\\dots ,n\\); then \\(a_1=1,\\;a_n=n\\).\n\n**Left‑hand critical columns.**  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the column\n\\[\nc_k=a_k-1.\n\\]\nThe column \\(c_k\\) is not a hole.  \nFor rows \\(i_j\\) with \\(j\\ge k\\) we have \\(c_k<a_j\\), so \\((i_j,c_k)\\) lies left of the hole in row \\(i_j\\); for rows \\(i_j\\) with \\(j<k\\) we have \\(c_k\\ge a_j\\), so \\((i_j,c_k)\\) is either a hole or lies right of the hole and therefore is not part of the left‑hand region.  \nConsequently the set\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(i_j,c_k):j\\ge k\\}\n\\]\nconsists exactly of the squares in column \\(c_k\\) that must be covered by tiles lying **entirely left** of the holes.\n\nLet a left‑hand tile be a rectangle \\(R\\times C\\) with \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,c\\}\\) for some column \\(c\\).  \nIf such a tile contains any square of \\(S_k\\), then its column interval must contain \\(c_k\\).  \nBecause the tile is left of the holes of all rows it covers, its rightmost column \\(c_{\\max}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nc_{\\max}<\\min_{i\\in R}\\pi(i).\n\\]\nAll rows in \\(R\\) belong to \\(\\{i_j:j\\ge k\\}\\), so \\(\\min_{i\\in R}\\pi(i)\\ge a_k\\).  \nHence \\(c_{\\max}<a_k\\), and since the tile contains column \\(c_k=a_k-1\\) we obtain \\(c_{\\max}=c_k\\).  \nThus **every left‑hand tile that covers any square of \\(S_k\\) has its rightmost column exactly \\(c_k\\).**  \nTwo different values of \\(k\\) give different rightmost columns, so the sets \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_n\\) require at least one distinct left‑hand tile each.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) left‑hand tiles are necessary.\n\n**Right‑hand critical columns.**  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the column\n\\[\nd_k=a_k+1.\n\\]\nAnalogously, the squares\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(i_j,d_k):j\\le k\\}\n\\]\nare exactly those in column \\(d_k\\) that lie **right** of the holes.  \nA right‑hand tile covering any square of \\(T_k\\) must have its leftmost column equal to \\(d_k\\); otherwise it would contain a hole.  \nHence the sets \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_{n-1}\\) require at least one distinct right‑hand tile each, giving at least \\(n-1\\) right‑hand tiles.\n\nSumming the two parts,\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2n-2.\n\\]\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence Matilda can achieve the required condition with \\(4048\\) rectangular tiles, and no arrangement can do it with fewer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
