ifferent rows) and together cover exactly all squares of \\(M\\).  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The construction uses \\(N\\) tiles, and the lower‑bound argument shows that fewer than \\(N\\) tiles are impossible.  \n   - Therefore the minimum number of tiles is \\(N=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(N=2025\\).  \nNumber the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,N\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Algebraic description of a placement\n\nChoose a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\).  \nThe unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\); consequently each column also contains exactly one uncovered square.\n\nDefine the \\(N\\times N\\) binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nM=(m_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le N},\\qquad \nm_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if }j=p(i)\\\\[2pt]\n1 &\\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(M\\) has a zero exactly at the positions of the uncovered squares and a one elsewhere.  \nLet \\(J\\) be the all‑ones matrix and let \\(P\\) be the permutation matrix of \\(p\\) (i.e. \\(P_{ij}=1\\) iff \\(j=p(i)\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nM = J-P. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tile as a rank‑1 matrix\n\nConsider a rectangular tile whose set of rows is \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\) and whose set of columns is \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\).  \nThe tile is admissible precisely when \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\); otherwise it would contain a forbidden square \\((i,i)\\) for some \\(i\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nu^{(R)}_i=\\begin{cases}1,&i\\in R,\\\\0,&i\\notin R,\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nv^{(C)}_j=\\begin{cases}1,&j\\in C,\\\\0,&j\\notin C.\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe binary matrix of the tile equals the outer product  \n\n\\[\nA^{(R,C)} = u^{(R)}\\,(v^{(C)})^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich has rank \\(1\\) over the real numbers.\n\nIf a family of tiles \\(\\{(R_k,C_k)\\}_{k=1}^{t}\\) covers exactly the squares represented by \\(M\\) (no overlap, no uncovered square covered), then\n\n\\[\nM = \\sum_{k=1}^{t} A^{(R_k,C_k)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Rank of the matrix \\(M\\)\n\nWe claim that for **any** permutation matrix \\(P\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=N .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^N\\) satisfy \\((J-P)x=0\\).  \nWrite \\(s=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_i\\).  \nSince \\(Jx = s\\mathbf{1}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\), the equation becomes  \n\n\\[\ns\\mathbf{1}=Px .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nApply the row vector \\(\\mathbf{1}^{\\mathsf T}\\) to both sides of (5).  \nBecause \\(P\\) merely permutes the coordinates, \\(\\mathbf{1}^{\\mathsf T}Px = \\mathbf{1}^{\\mathsf T}x = s\\).  \nHence \\(s\\,N = s\\), i.e. \\(s(N-1)=0\\).  \nSince \\(N>1\\), we obtain \\(s=0\\).  \nWith \\(s=0\\), (5) reduces to \\(Px=0\\).  \nBut \\(P\\) is invertible (it is a permutation matrix), so \\(x=0\\).  \nThus the only solution of \\((J-P)x=0\\) is \\(x=0\\); consequently \\(J-P\\) is nonsingular and has full rank \\(N\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEach tile contributes a rank‑1 matrix (2).  \nThe sum of \\(t\\) rank‑1 matrices has rank at most \\(t\\).  \nFrom (3) and (4),\n\n\\[\nN=\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le \\operatorname{rank}\\!\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{t}A^{(R_k,C_k)}\\Bigr)\n\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(A^{(R_k,C_k)}) = t .\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible tiling must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge N .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(N\\) tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad C_i=\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe tile occupies the