the squares represented by \\(M\\) (no overlap, no uncovered square covered), then\n\n\\[\nM = \\sum_{k=1}^{t} A^{(R_k,C_k)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Rank of the matrix \\(M\\)\n\nWe claim that for **any** permutation matrix \\(P\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=N .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^N\\) satisfy \\((J-P)x=0\\).  \nWrite \\(s=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_i\\).  \nSince \\(Jx = s\\mathbf{1}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\), the equation becomes  \n\n\\[\ns\\mathbf{1}=Px .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nApply the row vector \\(\\mathbf{1}^{\\mathsf T}\\) to both sides of (5).  \nBecause \\(P\\) merely permutes the coordinates, \\(\\mathbf{1}^{\\mathsf T}Px = \\mathbf{1}^{\\mathsf T}x = s\\).  \nHence \\(s\\,N = s\\), i.e. \\(s(N-1)=0\\).  \nSince \\(N>1\\), we obtain \\(s=0\\).  \nWith \\(s=0\\), (5) reduces to \\(Px=0\\).  \nBut \\(P\\) is invertible (it is a permutation matrix), so \\(x=0\\).  \nThus the only solution of \\((J-P)x=0\\) is \\(x=0\\); consequently \\(J-P\\) is nonsingular and has full rank \\(N\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEach tile contributes a rank‑1 matrix (2).  \nThe sum of \\(t\\) rank‑1 matrices has rank at most \\(t\\).  \nFrom (3) and (4),\n\n\\[\nN=\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le \\operatorname{rank}\\!\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{t}A^{(R_k,C_k)}\\Bigr)\n\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(A^{(R_k,C_k)}) = t .\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible tiling must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge N .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(N\\) tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad C_i=\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe tile occupies the rectangle \\(\\{i\\}\\times C_i\\), i.e. the whole row except the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\).  \nDifferent rows give disjoint rectangles, so the “at most one tile per unit square’’ condition is satisfied.  \nEvery row contains exactly one uncovered square (its diagonal entry), and every column also contains exactly one uncovered square (the same diagonal entry).  \nThus we have constructed a valid placement using precisely \\(N\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality\n\nThe construction uses \\(N\\) tiles, and the lower bound (6) shows that no placement can use fewer than \\(N\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(N\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(N=2025\\), the minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
