--------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to a bipartite edge‑partition  \n\nRemove the squares of \\(U\\) from the board.  The remaining squares correspond to the edge set of the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{n,n}\\) with the perfect matching  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{\\,\\{i,\\pi(i)\\}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ndeleted.  A tile \\(R\\times C\\) is precisely the edge set of the complete bipartite subgraph \\(K_{R,C}\\).  \nThus the problem asks for the smallest integer \\(k\\) for which there exist subsets  \n\n\\[\n(R_t,C_t)\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k),\\qquad R_t\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;C_t\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n* \\(R_t\\cap C_t=\\varnothing\\) for every \\(t\\) (otherwise a diagonal edge would be covered),  \n* the family \\(\\{K_{R_t,C_t}\\}_{t=1}^k\\) partitions the edge set \\(E(K_{n,n})\\setminus M\\).\n\nDenote the minimal possible \\(k\\) by \\(f(n)\\).  Our goal is to determine \\(f(2025)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound: a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nBecause the value of \\(f(n)\\) is invariant under simultaneous permutations of rows and columns, we may assume \\(\\pi\\) is the identity, i.e. the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nDefine the following \\(2n-2\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_i&=\\{(r,i)\\mid r=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n-1,\\\\[4pt]\nS_j&=\\{(r,j)\\mid r=1,2,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\qquad j=2,\\dots ,n.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(T_i\\) is a vertical strip in column \\(i\\) covering all rows strictly below the diagonal; each \\(S_j\\) is a vertical strip in column \\(j\\) covering all rows strictly above the diagonal.*\n\n**Disjointness.**  \nAll rectangles in \\(\\{T_i\\}\\) have distinct columns, so they are pairwise disjoint.  The same holds for \\(\\{S_j\\}\\).  Moreover a rectangle \\(T_i\\) uses column \\(i\\) while a rectangle \\(S_j\\) uses column \\(j\\); since \\(i\\neq j\\) the two families never intersect.  Hence the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any off‑diagonal cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r\\neq c\\).  \nIf \\(r>c\\) then \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(r\\ge c+1\\); thus \\((r,c)\\in T_c\\).  \nIf \\(r<c\\) then \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(r\\le c-1\\); thus \\((r,c)\\in S_c\\).  \nHence every off‑diagonal cell belongs to exactly one rectangle, while the diagonal cells are uncovered.\n\nConsequently a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists, so\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le 2n-2.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nWe prove that any partition of the edges of \\(K_{n,n}\\setminus M\\) into complete bipartite subgraphs requires at least \\(2n-2\\) parts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  Vector representation (Graham–Pollak style)\n\nLet the \\(k\\) rectangles be \\(\\{R_t\\times C_t\\}_{t=1}^k\\).  \nFor each vertex \\(v\\) of the bipartite graph (i.e. each row \\(i\\) and each column \\(j\\)) define a vector \\(x_v\\in\\mathbb{R}^k\\) by  \n\n\\[\n(x_v)_t=\n\\begin{cases}\n+1 &\\text{if }v\\text{ is a row belonging to }R_t,\\\\[2pt]\n-1 &\\text{if }v\\text{ is a column belonging to }C_t,\\\\[2pt]\n0  &\\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(d_v:=x_v\\!\\cdot\\!x_v\\) be the number of rectangles containing \\(v\\).\n\n*Dot products.*  \nFor two distinct vertices \\(u\\neq v\\) the product \\((x_u)_t (x_v)_t\\) is \\(-1\\) exactly when the rectangle \\(t\\) contains the edge \\(uv\\) (i.e. when \\(u\\in R_t\\) and \\(v\\in C_t\\) or vice‑versa); otherwise the product is \\(0\\).  \nSince the rectangles partition the edge set, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx_u\\!\\cdot\\!x_v=\n\\begin{cases}\n-1 &\\text{if }uv\\in E(K_{n,n})\\setminus M,\\\\[2pt]\n0  &\\text{if }uv\\in M.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(2n\\times2n\\) Gram matrix \\(G=(g_{uv})\\) with entries \\(g_{uv}=x_u\\!\\cdot\\!x_v\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nG=D-J+P,\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n* \\(D=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\dots ,d_{2n})\\) is diagonal,\n* \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix,\n* \\(P\\) is the permutation matrix of the perfect matching \\(M\\) (the only non‑zero off‑diagonal entries of \\(G\\) are the \\(-1\\)’s; the entries corresponding to matched pairs are \\(0\\)).\n\nBecause \\(G\\) is a Gram matrix, it is positive semidefinite; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(G)\\le k.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2  Rank of \\(D+P\\)\n\nWrite \\(D+P\\) in block form.  The matching pairs the vertex \\(i\\) with vertex \\(\\pi(i)\\) (here \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) after the reduction of § 1).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nD+P=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nd_1 & 1 &        &        \\\\\n1   & d_{\\pi(1)} &   &        \\\\\n    &        & \\ddots &        \\\\\n    &        &        & d_{2n}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nis block‑diagonal with \\(n\\) blocks  \n\n\\[\nB_i=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nd_i & 1\\\\[2pt]\n1   & d_{\\pi(i)}\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA block \\(B_i\\) is singular exactly when \\(d_i d_{\\pi(i)}=1\\), i.e. when **both** vertices of the matched pair appear in precisely one rectangle.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\#\\{\\,i\\mid d_i=d_{\\pi(i)}=1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEach non‑singular block contributes rank 2, each singular block contributes rank 1; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(D+P)=2n-m.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.3  At most one singular block  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that two distinct matched pairs \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) and \\((j,\\pi(j))\\) satisfy \\(d_i=d_{\\pi(i)}=d_j=d_{\\pi(j)}=1\\).\n\n* Because \\(d_i=1\\), row \\(i\\) occurs in exactly one rectangle, say \\(R_{t}\\times C_{t}\\).  That rectangle must contain **all** edges incident with row \\(i\\), i.e. all columns except \\(\\pi(i)\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nR_{t}=\\{i\\},\\qquad C_{t}=[n]\\setminus\\{\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n* Similarly, \\(d_{\\pi(i)}=1\\) forces a unique rectangle covering column \\(\\pi(i)\\); it must contain all rows except \\(i\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nR_{s}=[n]\\setminus\\{i\\},\\qquad C_{s}=\\{\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning holds for the pair \\((j,\\pi(j))\\).  \nNow consider the cell \\((i,\\pi(j))\\).  \nIt belongs to the rectangle described in (15) (because \\(\\pi(j)\\neq\\pi(i)\\)) **and** to the rectangle described in (16) for column \\(\\pi(j)\\) (because \\(i\\neq j\\)).  \nThus the cell \\((i,\\pi(j))\\) would be covered twice, contradicting the requirement that each unit square be covered at most once.\n\nHence at most one matched pair can have both degrees equal to 1, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nm\\le 1.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.4  Final rank estimate  \n\nFrom (10) we have \\(G=(D+P)-J\\).  Since \\(J\\) has rank 1,  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(G)\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(D+P)-1.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nCombining (14) and (17) with (18) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(G)\\ge 2n-m-1\\ge 2n-2.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nFinally, (11) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(G)\\ge 2n-2.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom the construction of § 3 we have \\(f(n)\\le 2n-2\\); from the lower‑bound argument of § 4 we have \\(f(n)\\ge 2n-2\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the original board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.  ∎"
