onal.  \n   For each \\(\\ell=0,1,\\dots ,r-1\\) define two rectangles\n\n   \\[\n   A_{\\ell}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le 2^{\\ell},\\;2^{\\ell}<j\\le 2^{\\ell+1}\\},\n   \\qquad\n   B_{\\ell}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 2^{\\ell}<i\\le 2^{\\ell+1},\\;1\\le j\\le 2^{\\ell}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   * Each \\(A_{\\ell}\\) (resp. \\(B_{\\ell}\\)) is a genuine axis\u2011parallel rectangle: its rows and columns are consecutive intervals.  \n   * Because \\(i\\le 2^{\\ell}<j\\) (resp. \\(j\\le 2^{\\ell}<i\\)), no diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) belongs to any \\(A_{\\ell}\\) or \\(B_{\\ell}\\).  \n   * For any ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) let \\(\\ell\\) be the most significant bit in which the binary expansions of \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) differ.  \n     \u2013 If the bit of \\(i\\) is \\(0\\) and that of \\(j\\) is \\(1\\), then \\((i,j)\\in A_{\\ell}\\).  \n     \u2013 If the bit of \\(i\\) is \\(1\\) and that of \\(j\\) is \\(0\\), then \\((i,j)\\in B_{\\ell}\\).  \n\n     Hence each off\u2011diagonal cell belongs to exactly one of the \\(2r\\) rectangles; the rectangles are pairwise disjoint, so they form a tiling.\n\n   For a general \\(n\\) (not a power of two) let \\(r=\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) and apply the same construction to the \\(2^{r}\\times2^{r}\\) board, then delete the extra rows and columns.  Truncating a rectangle can only split it into smaller rectangles, never increasing the total number; therefore the same bound \\(2r\\) works for the original \\(n\\times n\\) board.\n\n4. **Numerical evaluation.**  \n  "
