ut k=r is not allowed. So the largest k in 0..r-1 such that i ≤ a_k is r-1 (since a_{r-1}=1024). So they claim j ≤ a_{k+1}. Since k+1 = r, a_{r}=n=2025, and j ≤ n is true. So (i,j) ∈ A_{r-1}? Let's check: A_{r-1} = rows 1..a_{r-1}=1024, columns a_{r-1}+1..a_{r}=2025. So any (i,j) with i ≤ 1024 and j > 1024 is covered. But what about i > 1024? For i=1500, i ≤ a_{r-1}? No, i = 1500 > a_{r-1}=1024, so i ≤ a_{r-1} fails. Then we need to find largest k such that i ≤ a_k. Since a_{r}=2025, i ≤ a_{r} holds, but k must be ≤ r-1? Actually they said largest index such that i ≤ a_k. If i > a_{r-1}, then the only a_k that satisfy i ≤ a_k is a_{r}=2025, but k=r is not allowed. So the largest k such that i ≤ a_k among 0..r-1 does not exist. So the algorithm fails for i > a_{r-1}. However, they might have meant \"largest k such that i ≤ a_k\" where a_k is defined for k from 0..r (including a_r = n). Then k can be r, but then A_k is defined only for k=0..r-1. So for i > a_{r-1}, we need to consider k = r-1? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe region U includes all pairs (i,j) with i<j. The rectangles A_k are defined for k from 0 to r-1. For k = r-1, A_{r-1} covers rows 1..a_{r-1}, columns a_{r-1}+1..a_{r} (i.e., rows up to 1024, columns 1025..2025). So any (i,j) with i ≤ 1024 and j > 1024 is covered. But what about i > 1024? For i > 1024, we need to find a rectangle that covers (i,j). But A_{r-1} only includes rows up to 1024, so it doesn't cover i > 1024. However, for i > 1024, we can consider B_k rectangles? No, B_k are below diagonal, not above. So the construction appears to not cover all of U. Let's test with an example: n=2025, i=1500, j=1501. i<j, so (1500,1501) ∈ U. Is it covered by any A_k? For k=0, A_0 = rows 1..a_0=0 (empty) -> no. For k=1, rows 1..a_1=2, columns 3..4? Actually a_2=4, so A_1 = rows 1..2, columns 3..4. Not covering i=1500. For k=2, rows 1..4, columns 5..8. Not covering. For k up to r-2, rows up to a_{r-2}=512? Actually a_{9}=512, a_{10}=1024. So all A_k 