umber of the strict order is exactly ceil(log2 n). So the lower bound is tight. The construction uses binary representation: For each bit position k (0-indexed), define rectangle R_k = { (i,j) : i's k-th bit = 0, j's k-th bit = 1 }. This yields at most ceil(log2 n) rectangles that cover all pairs i<j. Indeed, if i<j, then there exists a most significant bit where i and j differ; i has 0 and j has 1 at that bit, so (i,j) lies in the rectangle for that bit. This yields a covering. The rectangles are not necessarily contiguous intervals in rows and columns; they are sets of rows and columns defined by bit patterns, which may not be contiguous, so they are not axis-aligned rectangles in the grid sense (they are combinatorial rectangles, i.e., product of sets of rows and columns, but not necessarily contiguous intervals). However, the problem requires rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines, i.e., contiguous intervals of rows and columns (i.e., axis-aligned rectangles). The bit-based rectangles may be discontiguous (they are unions of many small intervals). So they are not allowed. The problem requires each tile to be a contiguous rectangle. So the known combinatorial covering number result may not directly apply. The solution's construction attempts to use contiguous intervals.\n\nThus the minimal number of contiguous rectangles covering the strict upper triangular matrix may be larger than ceil(log2 n). Let's think about known results: The problem is similar to covering the upper triangular matrix with \"staircase\" rectangles that are contiguous intervals. The minimal number of such rectangles is known to be 2*ceil(log2 n). Actually, there is a known result: The minimum number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition the off-diagonal cells of an n x n matrix is exactly 2*ceil(log2 n). I recall a known problem: \"Cover the cells above the main diagonal of an n x n board with the fewest number of rectangles that do not intersect the diagonal.\" The answer 