re is known concept of \"grid decomposition\" or \"rectangular decomposition of a permutation matrix\". The complement of a permutation matrix is the set of all cells except those n cells. The complement can be partitioned into at most 2n-2 rectangles? Not sure.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles covering all cells except those n holes. This is similar to the problem of covering the complement of a permutation matrix with monotone rectangles that avoid the holes. The minimal number of rectangles needed is known to be something like n? Actually, consider the trivial solution: For each row, you could cover all cells in that row except the hole with one rectangle (if the hole is somewhere in the row, you can cover the left part and right part as two rectangles, but you could use two rectangles per row: one covering cells left of the hole, one covering cells right of the hole. Similarly for columns. But you can combine across rows to reduce count.\n\nAlternatively, you could cover each row's left side with a rectangle that extends across multiple rows, as long as there is no hole in that region. The holes are arranged like a permutation, so the region left of the hole in each row forms a Young diagram shape (if we sort rows by hole column positions). The complement of the permutation matrix is a union of two monotone shapes: the cells above the permutation (i.e., those with column index less than the hole's column for that row) and those below (i.e., column index greater than the hole's column). Actually, for each row i, suppose the hole is at column p(i). Then cells with column < p(i) are left of the hole; cells with column > p(i) are right of the hole. The set of left cells across rows forms a shape that is monotone decreasing: if we sort rows by i, the left region is a Young diagram shape (Ferrers diagram) defined by the permutation. Similarly the right region is the complement shape.\n\nIf we choose the diagonal (p(i)=i), then left region is cells with 