ction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\)).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le j-1\\). This tile lies in \\(A\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) for \\(j+1\\le i\\le n\\). This tile lies in \\(B\\).  \n\n   The two families are disjoint, together cover every non‑diagonal cell, and consist of \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\\) tiles. Each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered diagonal cell, so the required condition holds.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let \\(\\sigma(i)\\) be the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the uncovered square in column \\(j\\) is \\((\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The two admissible regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a rectangular tile \\(T\\) contained a cell of \\(A\\) and a cell of \\(B\\), then some row \\(i\\) of \\(T\\) would contain both a column larger than \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and a column smaller than \\(\\sigma(i)\\); because the columns of \\(T\\) form a contiguous interval, this would force the interval to contain \\(\\sigma(i)\\), i.e. the hole of row \\(i\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence **every tile lies entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).**\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Corner cells  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma(i)+1>\\sigma(i)\\), we have \\(c_i\\in A\\).  \nExactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); therefore the set \\(\\{c_i\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) cells.\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)<n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nd_j=(\\sigma^{-1}(j)+1,j).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)+1>\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) and \\(\\sigma(\\sigma^{-1}(j)+1)\\neq j\\), we obtain \\(j<\\sigma(\\sigma^{-1}(j)+1)\\); thus \\(d_j\\in B\\).  \nExactly one column has \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)=n\\); consequently there are also \\(n-1\\) cells \\(d_j\\).\n\nThe cells \\(c_i\\) are the **leftmost** cells of \\(A\\) in their rows, and the cells \\(d_j\\) are the **topmost** cells of \\(B\\) in their columns.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Each tile can contain at most one corner  \n\nConsider a tile \\(T\\subseteq A\\).  \nLet the rows of \\(T\\) be a contiguous interval \\(I\\) and its columns a contiguous interval \\(J=[\\ell,r]\\).  \nSince \\(T\\subseteq A\\), for every \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(\\ell>\\sigma(i)\\); in particular \\(\\ell>\\max_{i\\in I}\\sigma(i)\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contained two distinct corner cells \\(c_{i_1}\\) and \\(c_{i_2}\\) (with \\(i_1\\neq i_2\\)), then  \n\n\\[\n\\ell=\\sigma(i_1)+1=\\sigma(i_2)+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich would imply \\(\\sigma(i_1)=\\sigma(i_2)\\), contradicting that \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nHence **\\(T\\) contains at most one cell \\(c_i\\).**  \n\nThe same argument applied to a tile \\(T\\subseteq B\\) (now using the rightmost column of \\(T\\) and the fact that all its columns satisfy \\(r<\\sigma(i)\\) for the rows \\(i\\) of \\(T\\)) shows that **\\(T\\) contains at most one cell \\(d_j\\).**\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nAll cells \\(c_i\\) belong to \\(A\\) and must be covered by tiles lying in \\(A\\); by the previous paragraph each such tile covers at most one of them.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(A\\).  \nAnalogously, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(B\\).  \n\nConsequently any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the region \\(A\\) (cells with column \\(>i\\)):*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i<j\\), each cell of \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\) satisfies \\(j>\\sigma(i)=i\\); thus \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\subseteq A\\).\n\n*For the region \\(B\\) (cells with column \\(<i\\)):*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nHere \\(i>j\\), so \\(j<\\sigma(i)=i\\); hence \\(T_j^{\\text{down}}\\subseteq B\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint (they occupy different columns) and together cover every non‑diagonal cell of the board.  \nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{T_j^{\\text{up}} : 2\\le j\\le n\\}|+| \\{T_j^{\\text{down}} : 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nFinally, each row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) and is otherwise completely covered by the vertical tiles of the other columns; similarly each column \\(j\\) contains exactly the uncovered cell \\((j,j)\\).  \nThus the construction satisfies all requirements with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nEquation \\((1)\\) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, while the construction above shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
