n attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\)).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le j-1\\). This tile lies in \\(A\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) for \\(j+1\\le i\\le n\\). This tile lies in \\(B\\).  \n\n   The two families are disjoint, together cover every non‑diagonal cell, and consist of \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\\) tiles. Each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered diagonal cell, so the required condition holds.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let \\(\\sigma(i)\\) be the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the uncovered square in column \\(j\\) is \\((\\sigma^{-1}(j),j)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The two admissible regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a rectangular tile \\(T\\) contained a cell of \\(A\\) and a cell of \\(B\\), then some row \\(i\\) of \\(T\\) would contain both a column larger than \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and a column smaller than \\(\\sigma(i)\\); because the columns of \\(T\\) form a contiguous interval, this would force the interval to contain \\(\\sigma(i)\\), i.e. the hole of row \\(i\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence **every tile lies entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).**\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Corner cells  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\sigma(i)