\n     \\[\n     2(n-1)\n     \\]\n     tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   - Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     T_j^{\\uparrow}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n     \\]\n     which lies in \\(A\\) and contains the corner cell \\(c_{j-1}=(j-1,j)\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     T_j^{\\downarrow}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n     which lies in \\(B\\) and contains the corner cell \\(f_{j+1}=(j+1,j)\\).  \n   - The two families occupy distinct columns, are disjoint, and together cover every non\u2011diagonal cell.  \n   - The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \n\n#### 1.  Permutation description of the holes  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let \\(\\sigma(i)\\) denote the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.  \nHence the uncovered squares are precisely the cells  \n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  Two admissible regions  \n\nDefine  \n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.*  Every rectangular tile lies entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a tile \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i,j_1)\\) with \\(j_1>\\sigma(i)\\) (hence in \\(A\\)) and a cell \\((i,j_2)\\) with \\(j_2<\\sigma(i)\\) (hence in \\(B\\)).  \nSince a tile is a rectangle, its set of columns is a contiguous interval \\([L,R]\\) of integers containing both \\(j_1\\) and \\(j_2\\).  \nConsequently \\([L,R]\\) also contains \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nThus the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in H\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the condition that no unit square is covered by more than one tile. \u220e  \n\nHence the collection of tiles splits into two disjoint sub\u2011collections: those contained in \\(A\\) and those contained in \\(B\\).\n\n#### 3.  Corner cells of the two regions  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define  \n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n\\]  \nBecause \\(\\sigma(i)+1>\\sigma(i)\\), we have \\(c_i\\in A\\).  \nExactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); therefore the set  \n\\[\nC=\\{c_i\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\}\n\\]  \ncontains precisely \\(n-1\\) cells.  Each \\(c_i\\) is the left\u2011most cell of \\(A\\) in its row.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) define  \n\\[\nf_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1).\n\\]  \nSince \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(i)\\), we have \\(f_i\\in B\\).  \nExactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); consequently the set  \n\\[\nF=\\{f_i\\mid \\sigma(i)>1\\}\n\\]  \nalso contains exactly \\(n-1\\) cells.  Each \\(f_i\\) is the right\u2011most cell of \\(B\\) in its row.\n\n#### 4.  At most one corner per tile  \n\n*Lemma 1.*  A tile contained in \\(A\\) can contain at most one cell of \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\(T\\subseteq A\\) be a tile.  Its column set is a contiguous interval \\([L,R]\\).  \nAssume that \\(T\\) contains two distinct cells \\(c_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) and \\(c_{i'}=(i',\\sigma(i')+1)\\) with \\(i\\neq i'\\).  \nThen both \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')+1\\) belong to \\([L,R]\\).  \nWithout loss of generality suppose \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\).  \nSince \\([L,R]\\) contains the integers \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')+1\\), it also contains every integer between them, in particular \\(\\sigma(i')\\).  \nBut \\(\\sigma(i')\\) is the column of the hole \\((i',\\sigma(i'))\\), which would then lie in \\(T\\), contradicting the definition of a tile. \u220e  \n\n*Lemma 2.*  A tile contained in \\(B\\) can contain at most one cell of \\(F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The argument is identical to Lemma\u202f1, using the cells \\(f_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and the fact that the column interval of a tile containing two such cells would also contain \\(\\sigma(i)\\) or \\(\\sigma(i')\\), i.e. a hole. \u220e  \n\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nAll cells of \\(C\\) must be covered by tiles lying in \\(A\\); by Lemma\u202f1 each such tile covers at most one cell of \\(C\\).  \nHence at least \\(|C|=n-1\\) tiles are required in \\(A\\).\n\nAll cells of \\(F\\) must be covered by tiles lying in \\(B\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each such tile covers at most one cell of \\(F\\).  \nThus at least \\(|F|=n-1\\) tiles are required in \\(B\\).\n\nSince the two families of tiles are disjoint, any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\n*Tiles in \\(A\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\uparrow}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any cell \\((i,j)\\) in \\(T_j^{\\uparrow}\\) we have \\(j>i=\\sigma(i)\\); hence \\(T_j^{\\uparrow}\\subseteq A\\).  \nMoreover \\(T_j^{\\uparrow}\\) contains the corner cell \\(c_{j-1}=(j-1,j)\\).\n\n*Tiles in \\(B\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\downarrow}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any cell \\((i,j)\\) in \\(T_j^{\\downarrow}\\) we have \\(j<i=\\sigma(i)\\); thus \\(T_j^{\\downarrow}\\subseteq B\\).  \nEach \\(T_j^{\\downarrow}\\) contains the corner cell \\(f_{j+1}=(j+1,j)\\).\n\nThe two families \\(\\{T_j^{\\uparrow}\\}\\) and \\(\\{T_j^{\\downarrow}\\}\\) occupy distinct columns, so they are pairwise disjoint.  \nTogether they cover every non\u2011diagonal cell of the board.  \nEach row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) and is otherwise fully covered; similarly each column \\(j\\) contains the uncovered cell \\((j,j)\\) and is otherwise fully covered.  \n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{T_j^{\\uparrow}\\}|+|\\{T_j^{\\downarrow}\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (1) is attainable.\n\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nEquation (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, and the construction above achieves exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
