les: one above diagonal (if j>=2) and one below diagonal (if j <= n-1). Actually column j has T_j^up covering rows 1..j-1, and T_j^down covering rows j+1..n. The diagonal cell (j,j) is uncovered. So each column j has exactly one uncovered cell (the diagonal). So column condition satisfied. For each row i, row i has uncovered cell (i,i). Row i includes cells in columns j>i (i.e., to the right of diagonal) covered by T_j^up (since for j>i, T_j^up includes row i). Also includes cells in columns j<i (to left) covered by T_j^down (since for j<i, T_j^down includes row i). So all cells except diagonal are covered exactly once. So each row has exactly one uncovered cell. Tiles are rectangles (vertical strips). They do not overlap: T_j^up and T_k^up are in different columns; T_j^down and T_k^down are in different columns; T_j^up and T_k^down are in different columns; also T_j^up and T_j^down are disjoint because T_j^up covers rows 1..j-1, T_j^down covers rows j+1..n, leaving row j uncovered. So indeed they are disjoint. So the construction works. So answer 4048 is correct.\n\nThus Level 1: answer is correct.\n\nLevel 2: Check reasoning validity. Need to examine methods used: They used permutation representation, region splitting, case analysis, counting argument, lemmas about at most one corner per tile, lower bound via counting corners, explicit construction. These are valid mathematical methods: combinatorial reasoning, counting, constructive proof. Also they provided concrete details for construction (explicit sets of cells). So methods are valid. Also they gave concrete details for each tile: vertical tile definitions. So they satisfy Category C for construction claims. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Check presentation quality. Are there any justification gaps or critical errors? Let's examine the solution for potential gaps.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- The claim \"Every rectangular tile lies entirely in A or entirely in B.\" They provided proof: If tile contains cells