id 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThe *left region* is \\(L=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\) and the *right region* is \\(R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i\\).  \nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) are the two (possibly empty) contiguous blocks of squares in row \\(i\\) that must be covered.  \nSince a tile is a rectangle, it cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\): if a rectangle contained squares from both sides of the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) of some row \\(i\\), then it would also contain \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) itself, contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares remain uncovered. Consequently every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left region\n\nFor each row with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) let\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\n\\]\nbe the rightmost square of \\(L_i\\).  \nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a bijection, the set of columns \\(\\{\\pi(i)-1\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\}\\) equals \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus the points \\(\\{c_i\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\}\\) are \\(n-1\\) distinct squares, called the *left outer corners*.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\qquad\\text{Any rectangle }T\\subseteq L\\text{ contains at most one left outer corner.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T\\) occupy rows \\(i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\) and columns \\([c_1,c_2]\\) (with \\(c_1\\le c_2\\)).  \nSince \\(T\\subseteq L\\), for each occupied row \\(i_j\\) we have \\(c_2\\le\\pi(i_j)-1\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contained two distinct left outer corners \\((i_j,\\pi(i_j)-1)\\) and \\((i_{j'},\\pi(i_{j'})-1)\\), then both would have column \\(c_2\\); hence \\(\\pi(i_j)-1=c_2=\\pi(i_{j'})-1\\), implying \\(\\pi(i_j)=\\pi(i_{j'})\\), contradicting the injectivity of \\(\\pi\\). ∎\n\nEvery left outer corner must be covered by some tile, and each tile can cover at most one of them. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover the left regio