Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles satisfying \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the set of uncovered squares to be a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the uncovered unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal\n\nApply the same permutation \\(\\pi\\) to the rows and to the columns of the board: row \\(i\\) is renamed to row \\(\\pi(i)\\) and column \\(j\\) to column \\(\\pi(j)\\).  \nUnder this relabelling the set \\(U\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the main diagonal.  \nEvery tile, being an axis\u2011parallel rectangle, is sent to another axis\u2011parallel rectangle; the property \u201ceach unit square is covered by at most one tile\u2019\u2019 is preserved.  \nThus the minimal number of tiles for the original permutation \\(\\pi\\) equals the minimal number for the diagonal case (2).  \nHenceforth we assume that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of the diagonal case\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) is the set of cells strictly **below** the diagonal, \\(R\\) the set strictly **above** it.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus U = L\\cup R,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\nIf a rectangle \\(T\\) contains a cell of \\(L\\) and a cell of \\(R\\), then its row interval \\([r_{1},r_{2}]\\) and column interval \\([c_{1},c_{2}]\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nc_{2}\\ge i\\quad\\text{for some }(i,i-1)\\in L\\cap T,\n\\qquad\nc_{1}\\le j\\quad\\text{for some }(j,j+1)\\in R\\cap T.\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(c_{1}\\le c_{2}\\) and the diagonal cell \\((r_{1},r_{1})\\) (or \\((r_{2},r_{2})\\)) lies inside \\(T\\), contradicting (2).  \nTherefore **every admissible rectangle lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Corner squares\n\nFor each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,i-1)\\in L\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nis called a **left corner**; there are exactly \\(n-1\\) of them.  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nr_i=(i,i+1)\\in R\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nis a **right corner;** again there are \\(n-1\\) of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{A rectangle }T\\subseteq L\\text{ contains at most one left corner,}\\\\\n\\text{A rectangle }T\\subseteq R\\text{ contains at most one right corner.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider \\(T\\subseteq L\\).  \nLet its row interval be \\([r_{1},r_{k}]\\) (with \\(r_{1}<\\dots<r_{k}\\)) and its column interval \\([c_{1},c_{2}]\\).  \nBecause \\(T\\subseteq L\\), for every occupied row \\(r\\) we must have \\(c_{2}<r\\); otherwise column \\(c_{2}\\ge r\\) would place a cell on or to the right of the diagonal, contradicting \\(T\\subseteq L\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contained two distinct left corners \\((r_{a},r_{a}-1)\\) and \\((r_{b},r_{b}-1)\\) with \\(r_{a}<r_{b}\\), then \\(c_{2}\\ge r_{b}-1\\) (to contain the corner in row \\(r_{b}\\)) and simultaneously \\(c_{2}<r_{a}\\) (by the previous observation).  \nSince \\(r_{b}-1\\ge r_{a}\\), these two inequalities cannot hold together.  \nHence at most one left corner lies in \\(T\\).  \nThe argument for right corners in \\(R\\) is symmetric (now \\(c_{1}>r\\) for every occupied row). \u220e\n\nBecause a left corner must be covered by some tile and each tile can contain at most one left corner, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover all left corners.  \nThe same reasoning gives a further requirement of at least \\(n-1\\) tiles for the right corners.  \n\nA tile cannot contain a left corner **and** a right corner, for a left\u2011corner\u2011containing tile lies in \\(L\\) while a right\u2011corner\u2011containing tile lies in \\(R\\).  \nConsequently the total number of tiles needed is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1. Sharpness of the bound \u2013 a construction\n\nTake the diagonal permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \nFor each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich covers precisely the cells of row \\(i\\) that lie in \\(L\\).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the cells of row \\(i\\) that lie in \\(R\\).  \n\nThe families \\(\\{T_i^{\\text{L}}\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) and \\(\\{T_i^{\\text{R}}\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, each lies completely in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\), and none contains a diagonal cell.  \nTogether they cover every cell of the board except the diagonal cells (2).  \nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound (6) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe minimal number of rectangular tiles that can be placed on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
