tain the same Young diagram, now consisting of the cells\nright of the holes.  It is tiled by the \\(n-1\\) horizontal strips  \n\n\\[\nK_{k}= \\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\n\\qquad k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{H_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{K_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\)\ncover all squares that are not holes, they are pairwise disjoint, and each\nrow (resp. column) contains exactly one uncovered square, namely the hole\n\\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{there exists a tiling with }2n-2\\text{ tiles.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Critical cells – a lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)  \n\nFor every \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define two cells\n\n\\[\nA_{i}=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\qquad\\text{(the cell directly above the hole in row }i+1\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nB_{i}=(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\qquad\\text{(the cell directly below the hole in row }i\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the numbers \\(\\sigma(1),\\dots ,\\sigma(n)\\) are all distinct,\nthe \\(2(n-1)\\) cells \\(\\{A_{i},B_{i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) are pairwise\ndifferent.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  \nNo axis‑parallel rectangle that avoids all holes can contain two distinct\ncells among the set \\(\\{A_{i},B_{i}\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider any two distinct cells from the set.\n\n*Both are of type \\(A\\).*  \nSuppose the rectangle contains \\(A_{i}=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and\n\\(A_{j}=(j,\\sigma(j+1))\\) with \\(i<j\\).\nSince the rectangle’s row interval is contiguous, it also contains row \\(i+1\\).\nIts column interval contains both \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j+1)\\); in\nparticular it contains the column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\).\nConsequently the rectangle contains the hole \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\), a\ncontradiction.\n\n*Both are of type \\(B\\).*  \nThe argument is symmetric: if the rectangle contains\n\\(B_{i}=(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\) and \\(B_{j}=(j+