.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ determines a permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nAll other squares must be covered by axis‑aligned rectangles whose interiors contain no hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigl\\{(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\},\n\\qquad\nT=\\bigl\\{(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Each element of \\(S\\) lies in row \\(i\\) and column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\); each element of \\(T\\) lies in row \\(i+1\\) and column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, the rows of the elements of \\(S\\) are distinct, the columns of the elements of \\(S\\) are distinct, and similarly for \\(T\\).  \nMoreover \\(S\\cap T=\\varnothing\\) (otherwise \\(\\sigma\\) would not be injective).  \nThus \\(|S|=|T|=n-1\\) and \\(|S\\cup T|=2(n-1)\\).*\n\nLet a rectangle be \\(R=I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of consecutive integers (the rows and columns it occupies).  \nBecause a rectangle may not contain any hole, for every \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)\\notin J\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** *\\(R\\) can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.* Suppose \\(R\\) contains two distinct elements \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\((j,\\sigma(j+1))\\) of \\(S\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,j\\subseteq I\\), so in particular row \\(i+1\\in I\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\neq\\sigma(j+1)\\), the interval \\(J\\) contains both columns \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j+1)\\) and therefore contains the whole interval between them, which includes \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\).  \nConsequently the hole \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) lies in \\(I\\times J\\), contradicting the requirement that rectangles avoid holes. ∎\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.** *\\(R\\) can contain at most one element of \\(T\\).*\n\n*Proof.* The argument of Lemma 1 applied to the set \\(T\\) (replace each index \\(i\\) by \\(i-1\\)) yields the same conclusion. ∎\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 3.** *\\(R\\) cannot contain simultaneously an element of \\(S\\) and an element of \\(T\\).*\n\n*Proof.* Assume \\(R\\) contains \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\in S\\) and \\((j+1,\\sigma(j))\\in T\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(i\\le j\\).  \nThen rows \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,j+1\\subseteq I\\), so row \\(i+1\\in I\\).  \nBoth columns \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\) belong to \\(J\\); hence \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\in J\\).  \nThus the hole \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) lies in \\(R\\), a contradiction. ∎\n\nFrom Lemmas 1–3 we deduce:\n\n> **Corollary.** Every rectangle that avoids all holes can contain **at most one** element of the set \\(S\\cup T\\).\n\nSince \\(|S\\cup T|=2(n-1)\\), any tiling of the board must use at least \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the diagonal permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad\\text{(horizontal strip)},\n\\]\n\\[\nV_i=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{i\\}\\qquad\\text{(vertical strip)}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle, each \\(V_i\\) is a \\((n-i)\\times 1\\) rectangle; all sides lie on grid lines, so they are admissible tiles.\n\n*Disjointness.*  \n- Two horizontal strips \\(H_i\\) and \\(H_j\\) lie on different rows (\\(i\\neq j\\)), hence are disjoint.  \n- Two vertical strips \\(V_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) lie on different columns, hence are disjoint.  \n- A horizontal strip \\(H_i\\) and a vertical strip \\(V_j\\) would intersect at cell \\((i,j)\\).  \n  This cell belongs to \\(H_i\\) only if \\(j\\ge i+1\\), and belongs to \\(V_j\\) only if \\(i\\ge j+1\\).  \n  Both inequalities cannot hold simultaneously; therefore \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) are also disjoint.\n\n*Avoidance of holes.*  \nThe holes are the diagonal cells \\((k,k)\\).  \nEach \\(H_i\\) uses columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) and thus never includes column \\(i\\); each \\(V_i\\) uses rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) and never includes row \\(i\\).  \nHence no tile contains a diagonal cell.\n\n*Coverage of all off‑diagonal squares.*  \nLet \\((p,q)\\) be a square with \\(p\\neq q\\).  \n- If \\(p<q\\) then \\(q\\ge p+1\\), so \\((p,q)\\in H_p\\).  \n- If \\(p>q\\) then \\(p\\ge q+1\\), so \\((p,q)\\in V_q\\).\n\nThus every off‑diagonal square belongs to exactly one tile, and every diagonal square remains uncovered.\n\nThe construction uses precisely \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, i.e. \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing your output, carefully review your \"Summary\" and \"Detailed Solution\" sections to ensure they are clean, rigorous, and strictly adhere to all instructions provided above. Verify that every statement contributes directly to the final, coherent mathematical argument."
