sists of a single \\(0\\) (in column \\(\\sigma(i)\\)) and \\(1\\)’s elsewhere.  \n\nSuppose a linear relation  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_{i}\\,\\text{row}_{i}=0 \\qquad (\\alpha_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R})\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nholds.  \nLook at column \\(j\\). In column \\(j\\) the entry of row \\(i\\) equals \\(1\\) unless \\(\\sigma(i)=j\\); exactly one row, namely \\(i_{j}:=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\), has a \\(0\\) there.  \nHence the coefficient of column \\(j\\) in (4) is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\neq i_{j}}\\alpha_{i}=0 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) holds for every \\(j\\). Subtracting the equations for two different columns \\(j_{1},j_{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{i_{j_{2}}}-\\alpha_{i_{j_{1}}}=0,\n\\]\n\nso all \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) are equal: \\(\\alpha_{1}=\\alpha_{2}= \\dots =\\alpha_{n}=:\\alpha\\).  \nPlugging this into any equation (5) yields \\((n-1)\\alpha=0\\), whence \\(\\alpha=0\\).  \nThus the only linear relation among the rows is the trivial one; the rows are linearly independent, and consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nFrom (3) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTherefore any placement of tiles satisfying the conditions needs at least \\(2025\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_{j}= \\sigma^{-1}(j) \\qquad\\text{(the row containing the uncovered square in column }j\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nConsider the vertical strip  \n\n\\[\nT_{j}= \\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{r_{j}\\}\\bigr)\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_{j}\\) is a \\((n-1)\\times 1\\) rectangle; it contains every cell of column \\(j\\) except the uncovered cell \\((r_{j},j)\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) strips \\(\\{T_{j}\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all uncovered squares, and tog