rictly upper‑triangular)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(strictly lower‑triangular)}.\n\\]\n\nWe treat \\(U\\); the same reasoning applies to \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for covering \\(U\\)\n\nDefine the *corner cells* of \\(U\\) as  \n\n\\[\nC_U=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach cell of \\(C_U\\) is the south‑west corner of the Ferrers diagram that represents \\(U\\); there are exactly \\(n-1\\) of them.\n\nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be any rectangle contained in \\(U\\).  \nBecause \\(R\\subseteq U\\), (2) yields \\(\\max I<\\min J\\).  \nWrite \\(I=[a,b]\\) and \\(J=[c,d]\\) with \\(b<c\\).  \nA corner cell \\((i,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\) iff  \n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le b\\quad\\text{and}\\quad c\\le i+1\\le d .\n\\]\n\nThe two inequalities together give  \n\n\\[\nc-1\\le i\\le b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b<c\\), the interval \\([c-1,b]\\) contains **at most one integer**: it is either empty (if \\(c>b+1\\)) or the single integer \\(b\\) (if \\(c=b+1\\)).  \nHence **any rectangle in \\(U\\) contains at most one corner cell**.\n\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) corner cells are pairwise distinct, each must be covered by a (different) rectangle.  \nTherefore any partition of \\(U\\) into admissible rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The same bound for \\(L\\)\n\nThe lower‑triangular region \\(L\\) is the transpose of \\(U\\); its corner cells are  \n\n\\[\nC_L=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument shows that each admissible rectangle in \\(L\\) contains at most one element of \\(C_L\\); consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Overall lower bound\n\nAny tiling of the whole board must cover both \\(U\\) and \\(L\\).  \nHence the total number of tiles is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n*Upper‑triangular part.*  \nFor each row index \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}\n      =[i,i]\\times[i+1,n],\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(U\\).\n\n*Lower‑triangular part.*  \nFor each row index \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{low}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\}\n      =[i,i]\\times[1,i-1],\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (i-1)\\) tile.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\nNo rectangle contains a diagonal cell, because every \\(R_i^{\\text{up}}\\) satisfies \\(\\max I=i<\\min J=i+1\\) and every \\(R_i^{\\text{low}}\\) satisfies \\(\\max J=i-1<\\min I=i\\).  \nThus the construction respects the rule “each unit square is covered by at most one tile”.\n\nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nEquation (3) gives a universal lower bound; equation (4) provides a concrete tiling attaining that bound.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Numerical answer for the original problem\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\text{tiles}=2(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nMatilda can achieve the required condition with exactly \\(4048\\) rectangular tiles, and no arrangement can use fewer tiles. Hence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
