rgue each rectangle can contain at most one corner cell. The reasoning is correct.\n\nPotential justification gap: They assume that the rectangle's row interval I = [a,b] and column interval J = [c,d] with b<c. Then they say \"the interval [c-1,b] contains at most one integer: it is empty if c>b+1 and consists of the single integer b when c=b+1.\" That's correct. They might need to argue that b<c implies b ≤ c-1. That's fine.\n\nPotential gap: They claim that each corner cell must be covered by a different rectangle. This follows because each rectangle can contain at most one corner cell, and there are n-1 distinct corner cells. So each must be covered by a rectangle, but could a rectangle cover zero corner cells? Possibly, but then you'd need more rectangles to cover all corner cells. So minimal number of rectangles covering all cells must be at least n-1. That's correct.\n\nPotential gap: They need to argue that covering all cells of U requires covering all corner cells, but maybe some rectangle could cover non-corner cells without covering any corner cells, but still the total number of rectangles needed might be more than n-1. However lower bound is at least n-1, so it's fine.\n\nPotential gap: They treat L similarly.\n\nPotential gap: In the construction, they define R_i^up = [i,i] × [i+1,n]. That's a 1x(n-i) rectangle. They claim these are pairwise disjoint. Indeed, each rectangle occupies a distinct row i, so they are disjoint. Similarly for lower part.\n\nPotential gap: They claim \"No rectangle contains a diagonal cell, because every R_i^up satisfies max I = i < min J = i+1 and every R_i^low satisfies max J = i-1 < min I = i.\" That's correct.\n\nPotential gap: The solution uses \"horizontal rectangle\" but it's a 1-row rectangle; that's okay.\n\nPotential gap: They didn't explicitly state that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares. They assume that after renaming, the uncovered squares are diagonal. That's fine.\n\nPotential gap: The solution didn't discuss the possibility of using rectangles that span multiple rows (height >1). But they gave a lower bound that applies to any rectangle, regardless of height. So it's fine.\n\nPotential gap: The solution's lower bound argument uses \"corner cells\" which are defined as (i,i+1). They claim each rectangle contains at most one corner cell. This is correct. However, they need to argue that any rectangle covering any cell in U must contain at least one corner cell? Actually not: a rectangle could be placed entirely away from the diagonal, e.g., rows 1-2 and columns 5-6. That rectangle would not contain any corner cell (i,i+1). But the lower bound argument only says each rectangle can contain at most one corner cell, not that it must contain at least one. So the argument that at least n-1 rectangles are needed uses the fact that there are n-1 corner cells and each rectangle can contain at most one. However, if some rectangle contains zero corner cells, then you would need more rectangles to cover all corner cells. So the minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all cells of U must be at least n-1, because you need at least one rectangle per corner cell. This is correct: you cannot cover a corner cell without a rectangle that includes it; each rectangle can cover at most one corner cell, so you need at least as many rectangles as corner cells. That is a valid argument.\n\nPotential gap: The solution didn't explicitly argue that each corner cell must be covered by some rectangle. But it's obvious because all cells except diagonal must be covered. So it's fine.\n\nPotential gap: The solution didn't discuss the possibility of using rectangles that cover both upper and lower triangular region (i.e., crossing diagonal). But they already argued that any rectangle cannot intersect diagonal, so cannot cross. So it's fine.\n\nPotential gap: The solution didn't discuss the possibility of using rectangles that are not horizontal (height >1). The lower bound argument