;J-I_{\\sigma},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n* \\(J\\) is the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix, and  \n* \\(I_{\\sigma}\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\), i.e. \\((I_{\\sigma})_{i,j}=1\\) iff \\(j=\\sigma(i)\\).\n\nThus the matrix \\(J-I_{\\sigma}\\) (which has a \\(0\\) exactly at the uncovered squares and a \\(1\\) elsewhere) is expressed as a sum of \\(t\\) rank‑1 matrices.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Rank of \\(J-I_{\\sigma}\\)  \n\nThe eigenvalues of \\(J\\) are \\(n\\) (with eigenvector \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\)) and \\(0\\) (multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  \nSince \\(I_{\\sigma}\\) is a permutation matrix, it is orthogonal and has eigenvalues that are complex roots of unity; in particular it is invertible.  \n\nConsider the matrix\n\\[\nA:=J-I_{\\sigma}.\n\\]\nFor the vector \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) we have\n\\[\nA\\mathbf{1}=J\\mathbf{1}-I_{\\sigma}\\mathbf{1}=n\\mathbf{1}-\\mathbf{1}=(n-1)\\mathbf{1},\n\\]\nso \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) is an eigenvector with eigenvalue \\(n-1\\).  \nIf \\(x\\) satisfies \\(\\mathbf{1}^{\\mathsf T}x=0\\) (i.e. \\(x\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf{1}\\)), then \\(Jx=0\\) and consequently\n\\[\nAx=-I_{\\sigma}x.\n\\]\nSince \\(I_{\\sigma}\\) is invertible, \\(-I_{\\sigma}x\\neq0\\) for any non‑zero \\(x\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf{1}\\). Hence every such \\(x\\) is an eigenvector of \\(A\\) with eigenvalue \\(-1\\).  \n\nThus the spectrum of \\(A\\) consists of the eigenvalue \\(n-1\\) (multiplicity 1) and the eigenvalue \\(-1\\) (multiplicity \\(n-1\\)). All eigenvalues are non‑zero, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEach summand \\(u^{(k)}(v^{(k)})^{\\!\\mathsf T}\\) in (3) has rank at most 1.  \nThe rank of a sum of matrices does not exceed the sum of their ranks, therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\bigl(J-I_{\\sigma}\\bigr)\n   \\;\\le\\; \\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\bigl(u^{(k)}(v^{(k)})^{\\!\\mathsf T}\\bigr)\n   \\;\\le\\; t.\n\\]\nUsing (4) we obtain\n\\[\nt\\;\\ge\\;\\operatorname{rank}(J-I_{\\sigma})=n.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence **any** tiling satisfying the conditions must contain at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFix the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) that determines the uncovered squares.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a horizontal tile covering the set\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThis tile is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle; its row set is \\(\\{i\\}\\) and its column set is \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}\\), which satisfies condition (1).  \n\nThe \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows, and together they cover every square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones.  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (5) we have the universal lower bound \\(t\\ge n\\); the construction above shows that \\(t=n\\) is attainable. Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
