ell (i,a) ∈ L (so a < f(i)) and cell (j,b) ∈ R (so b > f(j)). T is a rectangle, so its column interval is [c,d] where c ≤ a,b ≤ d. So c ≤ a and b ≤ d. So the column interval includes all columns between a and b (if a ≤ b) or between b and a (if b ≤ a). But we need to consider ordering of a and b. Since a < f(i) and b > f(j). Could a > b? Possibly if f(i) is large and f(j) is small. But a is left of f(i), b is right of f(j). So a could be > b if f(i) > a > b > f(j). For example, f(i) = 10, a = 9 (left of 10). f(j) = 5, b = 6 (right of 5). Then a = 9 > b = 6. So the column interval of T must include both 9 and 6, thus includes all columns from 6 to 9. Does that interval contain any uncovered column? Uncovered columns are at f(i) = 10 and f(j) = 5. The interval [6,9] does not include 10 or 5. So T might not contain any uncovered squares. So the claim that any tile intersecting both L and R must contain an uncovered square is false in general. However, maybe the tile must also contain the row of the uncovered square for one of those columns? Let's examine more precisely.\n\nGiven tile T covering (i,a) and (j,b). Since T is a rectangle, it covers all cells (r,c) where r ∈ [min(i,j), max(i,j)] and c ∈ [min(a,b), max(a,b)]. So T includes all rows between i and j and all columns between a and b.\n\nNow consider the uncovered squares. For each row r in the interval, the uncovered column is f(r). If any f(r) lies within the column interval [min(a,b), max(a,b)], then T would cover that uncovered square (r, f(r)), which is forbidden. So for T to avoid uncovered squares, it must be that for all rows r between i and j, f(r) is not in the column interval.\n\nNow is it possible for T to intersect both L and R while avoiding uncovered squares? Let's try to construct a counterexample. Suppose we have n=5. Let f be permutation: f(1)=5, f(2)=1, f(3)=3, f(4)=2, f(5)=4. So uncovered squares: (1,5), (2,1), (3,3), (4,2), (5,4). L region includes cells left of each uncovered column: For row 1, L1 empty (since f(1)=5, left part columns 1..4). Actually left part includes columns 1..4. Wait left part is j < f(i). So for row 1, left part includes columns 1..4. Right part empty. For row 2, left part empty (since f(2)=1), right part columns 2..5. For row 3, left part columns 1..2, right part columns 4..5. For row 4, left part columns 1..1, right part columns 3..5. For row 5, left part columns 1..3, right part columns 5..5.\n\nNow consider a tile that covers (row 3, column 2) ∈ L (since left part includes columns 1..2). And also covers (row 4, column 3) ∈ R (since right part includes columns 3..5). So a=2, b=3, i=3, j=4. The column interval is [2,3]. Row interval is [3,4]. So tile covers cells: (3,2), (3,3), (4,2), (4,3). Uncovered squares: (3,3) is uncovered! Indeed (3,3) is uncovered. So tile would contain uncovered square. That is not allowed. So this tile is not allowed.\n\nBut maybe we can find a tile that covers (row i, a) ∈ L and (row j, b) ∈ R but does not cover any uncovered squares. Let's try to find a scenario where the column interval does not include any f(r) for rows between i and j. Suppose we choose i=1, a=2 (since left part includes columns 1..4). Choose j=2, b=5 (right part includes columns 2..5). Then a=2, b=5, column interval [2,5]. Row interval [1,2]. Uncovered squares: (1,5) and (2,1). (1,5) is column 5, which is in interval, row 1 is in row interval, so tile includes (1,5) uncovered. So not allowed.\n\nTry i=4, a=1 (left part includes column 1). j=5, b=5 (right part includes column 5). Column interval [1,5] includes all columns, so includes uncovered squares for rows 4 and 5: (4,2) and (5,4). So not allowed.\n\nTry i=3, a=1 (left part includes column 1). j=5, b=5 (right part includes column 5). Column interval [1,5] includes uncovered squares for rows 3 (3,3), 4 (4,2), 5 (5,4). So not allowed.\n\nIt seems any tile that includes a left cell and a right cell will have column interval that includes at least one uncovered column for some row in the interval. But is it always true? Let's think more generally.\n\nSuppose we have two rows i and j, with uncovered columns f(i) and f(j). Suppose a < f(i) (so a ∈ L_i) and b > f(j) (so b ∈ R_j). Without loss of generality assume i ≤ j (if not, swap). Then tile's row interval includes rows i..j. For each row r in [i,j], uncovered column is f(r). The column interval is [min(a,b), max(a,b)]. Since a < f(i) and b > f(j), we have min(a,b) ≤ a < f(i) and max(a,b) ≥ b > f(j). So the column interval includes the interval from a up to b. Now consider the function f on rows i..j. Since f is a permutation, the values f(r) for r ∈ [i,j] are a set of distinct integers between 1 and n. Among them, there is at least one value that lies between a and b inclusive? Not necessarily. For example, suppose a=2, b=3, and f(i)=5, f(j)=1. Then the column interval [2,3] does not contain either 5 or 1. But there could be some other row r between i and j whose f(r) is 2 or 3. But we need to guarantee at least one f(r) lies in the interval. Is that guaranteed? Not obvious.\n\nConsider the ordering of f values relative to a and b. Since a < f(i) and b > f(j), we have a < f(i) and b > f(j). If f(i) > b, then f(i) > b > f(j). So f(i) > b > f(j). So f(i) > b and f(j) < a? Actually we only know a < f(i) and b > f(j). So it's possible that f(i) > b and f(j) < a. So both uncovered columns are outside the interval [a,b]. But there might be other rows r between i and j with f(r) inside [a,b]. However, it's not guaranteed.\n\nBut perhaps we can argue using the pigeonhole principle: Since there are j-i+1 rows in interval and the interval [a,b] contains b-a+1 columns. If the number of rows > number of columns, then by pigeonhole some f(r) must fall in interval. But if j-i+1 ≤ b-a+1, it's possible that all f(r) are outside interval. However, we need to consider that a and b are from L_i and R_j respectively, so a < f(i) and b > f(j). So a ≤ f(i)-1, b ≥ f(j)+1. So b-a ≥ (f(j)+1) - (f(i)-1) = f(j)-f(i)+2. If f(j) < f(i), then b-a ≥ (negative) +2 = something maybe small. So it's not guaranteed that b-a is large.\n\nThus Lemma 3 might be flawed: It asserts that any tile intersecting both L and R must contain an uncovered square, but we found a potential scenario where it might avoid uncovered squares if the uncovered columns for rows in the interval are all outside the column interval. However, we need to check if such scenario can happen given the definitions of L and R. Let's examine more systematically.\n\nDefine L = {(i,j): j < f(i)}. R = {(i,j): j > f(i)}. So L and R partition all squares except the uncovered squares (i,f(i)). For a tile T to intersect both L and R, there must exist (i,a) ∈ L and (j,b) ∈ R with i ≤ j (or j ≤ i). Then T's column interval includes [a,b] (if a ≤ b) or [b,a] (if b ≤ a). But we can assume a ≤ b without loss of generality (swap labeling of left and right cells). Actually if a > b, then we have a left cell with column a > b, but left cell must be left of its uncovered column, so a < f(i). And b is right of its uncovered column, so b > f(j). If a > b, then a > b > f(j). So a > f(j). But a is left of f(i), so a < f(i). So we have f(j) < b < a < f(i). So the interval [b,a] includes columns between b and a. The uncovered columns f(j) and f(i) are outside that interval (f(j) < b, f(i) > a). So the column interval does not contain f(i) or f(j). But could it contain some other f(r) for r between i and j? Possibly.\n\nThus the claim that any tile intersecting both L and R must contain an uncovered square is not trivial. Let's try to construct a concrete counterexample for a small n.\n\nWe need a permutation f such that there exist rows i < j and columns a < f(i) and b > f(j) with a > b? Actually we can assume a < b for simplicity. So we need a < b, a < f(i), b > f(j). And we need that for all rows r between i and j, f(r) is either ≤ a or ≥ b (i.e., not in (a,b)). That would avoid uncovered squares inside tile. Let's try to find such a permutation for n=5.\n\nWe need a < b. Let's choose a=2, b=4. So we need a row i with f(i) > 2 (so uncovered column >2). That's easy. And a row j with f(j) < 4 (so uncovered column <4). Actually we need b > f(j), so f(j) < 4. So we need f(i) > 2 and f(j) < 4. That's easy. Also we need that for all rows r between i and j, f(r) ≤ 2 or ≥ 4. So no f(r) equals 3. So we need to avoid uncovered column 3 in that interval.\n\nLet's try permutation f: row1: f(1)=5, row2: f(2)=1, row3: f(3)=4, row4: f(4)=2, row5: f(5)=3. Let's examine rows i=1 (f=5) and j=2 (f=1). a must be < f(i) =5, choose a=2 (left cell (1,2) ∈ L). b must be > f(j) =1, choose b=4 (right cell (2,4) ∈ R). So tile covering (1,2) and (2,4) would have column interval [2,4] and row interval [1,2]. Does it contain any uncovered squares? Uncovered squares are (1,5), (2,1), (3,4), (4,2), (5,3). In rows 1 and 2, uncovered squares are (1,5) column 5 not in [2,4]; (2,1) column 1 not in [2,4]. So tile does not contain uncovered squares for rows 1 and 2. However, tile also includes rows 1 and 2 only, not row 3,4,5. So tile does not intersect any uncovered squares. So this tile intersects L (via (1,2)) and R (via (2,4)) but does not contain any uncovered squares. So Lemma 3 is false.\n\nBut is (2,4) indeed in R? For row2, f(2)=1, so R2 includes columns >1, i.e., 2..5. So (2,4) ∈ R. Yes. So tile covering (1,2) and (2,4) is a rectangle with rows 1..2 and columns 2..4. This rectangle includes cells: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). None of these are uncovered squares. So indeed tile intersects both L and R but does not contain uncovered squares. So Lemma 3 is false.\n\nThus the lower bound proof is flawed because it relies on Lemma 3 to argue that tiles cannot cross between L and R, which is false. However, perhaps the lower bound can still hold even if Lemma 3 is false? Let's examine the lower bound argument: They claim any tile lies entirely within L or entirely within R. If that's false, then the lower bound argument fails: maybe we can tile L and R with fewer tiles by using tiles that cross between them. For example, a tile that covers some left cells and some right cells could reduce tile count. The solution's lower bound uses that each tile can be counted either for left region or right region, so total tiles at least sum of lower bounds for each region. If tiles can cross, then the total number of tiles needed could be less than sum of lower bounds for each region because some tile can cover both left and right cells, counting for both but only one tile. So the lower bound might be too high.\n\nThus we need to check if the claimed minimum 2n-2 is indeed correct or not. Perhaps the true minimum is lower. Let's think about the problem more generally: We have a permutation matrix of uncovered squares. We need to tile the rest of the grid with rectangles that avoid those squares. The question: what is the minimum number of rectangles needed? The solution's construction yields 2n-2. Could there be a construction with fewer than 2n-2? Let's explore possibilities.\n\nWe need to tile the complement of the permutation matrix. This is a known problem: covering a grid with rectangles avoiding a permutation pattern. The complement of a permutation matrix is a set of cells that can be partitioned into monotone regions. There's known result that the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is n + (c-1) where c is number of cycles? Not sure.\n\nLet's think about the structure: The uncovered squares are like a rook placement (non-attacking rooks). The complement consists of all cells not on those rooks. We want to partition the complement into axis-aligned rectangles that avoid the rooks.\n\nOne simple approach: For each row, split into left and right intervals as solution does. That's 2n-2 tiles. But maybe we can combine some left intervals across rows into larger rectangles, reducing tile count. For example, consider rows i and i+1 where f(i) and f(i+1) are such that left intervals align: Suppose f(i) > f(i+1). Then left intervals L_i = columns 1..f(i)-1, L_{i+1} = columns 1..f(i+1)-1. Since f(i+1) < f(i), L_{i+1} is a subset of L_i. So we could tile the region formed by columns 1..f(i+1)-1 across rows i..i+1 as a rectangle, covering part of both left intervals. But we must avoid the uncovered squares (i,f(i)) and (i+1,f(i+1)). The rectangle covering columns 1..f(i+1)-1 across rows i..i+1 does not include those uncovered squares because those are at columns f(i) and f(i+1) which are > f(i+1)-1. So we can combine left intervals across rows into larger rectangles. Similarly for right intervals.\n\nThus we might be able to reduce tile count below 2n-2. The solution's lower bound fails because it assumes each tile can only be in L or R and each tile can be assigned a unique rightmost column (or leftmost column) for each k, but if a tile can cover multiple rightmost cells for different rows, the argument fails. Actually Lemma 1's argument that each tile has a unique rightmost column and thus each k requires a distinct tile is correct only if each tile can have only one rightmost column. But a tile can have a rightmost column k and cover multiple rows' rightmost cells (i_k, k). For each k, there is a unique row i_k with f(i_k)=k+1. So the rightmost cell of left region for that row is (i_k, k). If a tile has rightmost column k, it could cover (i_k, k) and also other left region cells in other rows that also have rightmost column k? But for any other row i', the rightmost column of its left region is f(i')-1. That could also be k if f(i') = k+1. But f is a permutation, so only one row has f(i') = k+1. So only one row has rightmost column k. So each tile with rightmost column k can cover only that one cell (i_k, k) as the rightmost cell of left region. However, the tile could also cover other left region cells that are not rightmost in their rows, i.e., cells with column < k. That's okay. So each tile can be assigned to at most one k. So the lower bound for left region holds even if tiles can cross between rows? Actually Lemma 1's argument does not rely on tile being confined to left region only; it only uses the fact that any tile covering (i_k, k) cannot extend to column k+1, so its rightmost column is k. So each tile covering that cell has rightmost column k. Since each tile can have only one rightmost column, distinct k require distinct tiles. This argument holds even if tile also covers some right region cells? Wait if tile also covers some right region cells, then it would intersect both L and R, which we previously argued is possible. But does that affect the argument? If a tile covers (i_k, k) and also covers some right region cell (j,b) with b > f(j), then tile's column interval includes both k and b. Since b > k, the tile's rightmost column would be at least b, not k. But we argued tile's rightmost column must be exactly k because it cannot extend to column k+1. However, if tile also includes column b > k+1, then it would extend beyond column k+1, which would include column k+1 (since column interval is contiguous). So tile would contain column k+1, which is the column of uncovered square (i_k, k+1). That would be forbidden. So tile cannot contain any column > k, because that would include column k+1. Therefore tile cannot intersect any right region cell with column > k. So any tile covering (i_k, k) cannot intersect any right region cell. So it must be wholly within L. So Lemma 1 ensures that any tile covering the rightmost cell of left region for a given k cannot intersect R. So each such tile is confined to L. So the lower bound for L holds.\n\nSimilarly, Lemma 2 ensures tiles covering leftmost cell of right region cannot intersect L. So each tile used for covering those cells is confined to R.\n\nThus the lower bound argument might still be valid even if Lemma 3 is false in general, because the specific tiles needed to achieve the lower bound for L and R are forced to stay within L and R. However, the lower bound argument uses Lemma 3 to claim that any tile lies entirely within L or R, which is not true. But they might not need that for the lower bound: they need to count tiles needed to cover L and R. If a tile can cover cells from both L and R, then it could potentially reduce the total number of tiles needed to cover L and R combined. But the lower bound argument counts tiles needed to cover L and R separately, assuming no tile can be counted for both. If a tile can intersect both L and R, then it could serve as a tile for covering some left cells and some right cells, reducing total count. However, Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 each guarantee that some tiles (those covering rightmost left cells and leftmost right cells) must be confined to L or R. But maybe we can cover the rest of L and R using tiles that cross between them, potentially reducing the total count below 2n-2. However, the lower bound argument still counts at least n-1 tiles for L and at least n-1 tiles for R. But if some tile can cover both a left cell and a right cell, it might reduce the total number of tiles needed for L and R combined, but does it reduce the count of tiles needed for L? No, because the tiles needed for L are at least n-1, regardless of whether some other tiles also cover some left cells. The lower bound for L says any tiling of L (i.e., covering all cells in L) requires at least n-1 tiles. However, if we use a tile that also covers some right cells, that tile also covers some left cells, so it counts as a tile covering left cells. So the lower bound for L still applies: we need at least n-1 tiles that cover left cells (including those that also cover right cells). Similarly, we need at least n-1 tiles that cover right cells. However, a single tile could be counted both as covering left cells and right cells, i.e., it could be used to satisfy both lower bounds simultaneously. But the lower bound argument adds the two lower bounds, assuming they are disjoint sets of tiles. If a tile can serve both purposes, the sum might overcount. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may not hold.\n\nThus we need to examine whether there exists a tiling using fewer than 2n-2 tiles. Let's try to find a construction for small n to see if it's possible.\n\nTake n=2. Grid 2x2. Need each row and column have exactly one uncovered square. So we need exactly 2 uncovered squares, one per row and column. So uncovered squares must be at positions (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's consider uncovered squares at (1,1) and (2,2). Then the remaining squares are (1,2) and (2,1). They are not adjacent horizontally or vertically; they are diagonal. Can we cover them with a single rectangular tile? A rectangle covering (1,2) and (2,1) would need to cover rows 1..2 and columns 1..2, i.e., the whole 2x2 grid, which would include uncovered squares, not allowed. So we need two tiles, each covering one of the remaining squares. So minimum tiles = 2. According to formula 2n-2 = 2*2-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nn=3. Let's test. Need 3 uncovered squares, one per row and column. Let's try a permutation f: (1,1), (2,2), (3,3) (the identity). Then uncovered squares are diagonal. The remaining squares are all off-diagonal cells: (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). Can we tile them with fewer than 2n-2 = 4 tiles? Let's see if we can tile with 3 tiles. Try to combine some cells. For example, tile covering row1 columns 2-3: that's a 1x2 tile covering (1,2),(1,3). Tile covering row2 columns 1 and 3: not contiguous, can't be one tile. Could tile covering column1 rows 2-3: rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1). That's a 2x1 tile. Then tile covering (2,3),(3,2) maybe not contiguous. Actually after covering (1,2),(1,3) and (2,1),(3,1), remaining cells are (2,3) and (3,2). Those are not in same row or column, can't be covered by a single rectangle without covering uncovered squares. So need two more tiles, total 4. So 4 tiles needed. Could we do better with different permutation