ows? The tile is a rectangle, so its column interval is same across all rows it covers. If the tile extends beyond column k+1, then in row i_k it would cover column k+1 which is uncovered. So can't. So correct.\n\nSimilarly for Lemma 2.\n\nLemma 3: They argue a rectangle containing squares from both L and R would have to contain uncovered square in some row. The proof seems correct.\n\nPotential justification gaps: The solution claims \"Since f is a permutation, there is exactly one row with f(i)=1 (its left part is empty) and exactly one row with f(i)=n (its right part is empty).\" That's correct. But they didn't explicitly argue that there cannot be more than one row with f(i)=1 because f is bijection. That's fine.\n\nThe solution also says \"Because a tile has a unique rightmost column, distinct values of k require distinct tiles.\" That's valid.\n\nPotential missing detail: The solution didn't explicitly argue that the left region L and right region R are disjoint and cover all squares except uncovered ones. That's implicit.\n\nThe solution uses \"Since a tile has a unique leftmost column\" etc. That's valid.\n\nOverall, presentation seems rigorous enough. No critical errors. Possibly minor justification gaps: The solution uses \"Thus for each k there must exist a tile whose rightmost column equals k.\" This is justified by the previous argument. It's fine.\n\nThus Level 3: No critical errors, maybe some minor justification gaps but those are acceptable.\n\nThus final verdict PASS.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON with fields: verdict, confidence, issues, answer_correctness, reasoning.\n\nWe need to list any issues found. Since we have no critical errors, but maybe we can note any justification gaps. But we can also leave issues empty if none. The instructions: \"issues\": [] list any presentation issues found (Justification Gaps or Critical Errors). If there are none, we can put empty array. If there are some justification gaps, we should list them. Let's see if 