d b > f(j). So we have a < f(i) and b > f(j). So f(i) > a, f(j) < b. So f(i) might be > b (if b < f(i)), but we have b > f(j). So f(j) < b. So f(j) could be less than a or between a and b. If f(j) < a, then tile's column interval [c,d] includes a..b, and f(j) is less than a, so uncovered square (j,f(j)) is not covered. That's okay. But we also need to consider row i: f(i) > a. If f(i) > b, then uncovered square (i,f(i)) is not covered. So tile could avoid covering uncovered squares in both rows i and j if f(i) > b and f(j) < a. Is that possible? Let's think: a is a column in left region of row i, so a < f(i). b is a column in right region of row j, so b > f(j). So we need f(i) > b > f(j). So we need f(i) > f(j). That's possible. So tile could be placed covering left region cell (i,a) and right region cell (j,b) such that f(i) > b > f(j). Then tile's column interval includes columns a..b, which are all less than f(i) (since b < f(i)). So uncovered square (i,f(i)) not covered. For row j, uncovered square (j,f(j)) is less than a (if f(j) < a). So not covered. So tile could avoid covering uncovered squares. However, the tile also covers rows between i and j (if i < j). For each such row r, we need to avoid f(r). The tile's column interval includes columns a..b. For each row r, if f(r) lies between a and b, uncovered square would be covered. So to avoid that, we need f(r) not in [a,b] for all rows r covered. Is that possible? Possibly if the permutation f is such that the values f(r) are all either <= a or >= b for rows r in the interval. But is that possible? Let's examine the structure: The tile's rows interval is some set of consecutive rows. The permutation f maps each row to a column. Could there be a rectangle that covers a left region cell and a right region cell but avoids all uncovered squares? Possibly, but we need to check if the argument that no tile can intersect both regions holds in general. Let's test with a small example: n=3. Suppose f = identity: uncovered squares at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Left region: cells left of each uncovered: row1 left region empty, row2 left region (2,1), row3 left region (3,1),(3,2). Right region: row1 right region (1,2),(1,3), row2 right region (2,3), row3 right region empty. Could we have a tile that covers a left region cell (say (3,2)) and a right region cell (say (1,2))? That tile would need to cover rows 1..3 and columns 2..2? Actually rectangle covering (3,2) and (1,2) would be rows 1..3, column 2 only (height 3, width 1). That rectangle includes (2,2) which is uncovered (since uncovered at (2,2)). So not allowed. Could we have tile covering (3,1) (left region) and (2,3) (right region)? That rectangle would be rows 2..3, columns 1..3. That includes (2,2) uncovered, (3,2) left region but that's okay, but includes (2,2) uncovered. So not allowed. Could we have tile covering (2,1) left region and (1,3) right region? That rectangle rows 1..2, columns 1..3 includes (1,1) uncovered? Actually uncovered at (1,1) is left region? Wait (1,1) is uncovered, left region empty for row1. So rectangle includes (1,1) uncovered. So not allowed. So in this example, no tile can intersect both left and right region. But is that always true? Let's try a different permutation: n=4, f = (1->4, 2->1, 3->3, 4->2). Let's list uncovered squares: (1,4), (2,1), (3,3), (4,2). Left region: row1 left region columns 1..3, row2 left region empty (since f=1), row3 left region columns 1..2, row4 left region column 1. Right region: row1 right region empty (since f=4), row2 right region columns 2..4, row3 right region column 4, row4 right region columns 3..4. Could we have a tile covering left region cell (1,2) and right region cell (2,4)? That rectangle rows 1..2, columns 2..4 includes (1,4) uncovered? Actually uncovered at (1,4) is column 4, row1. The rectangle includes column 4, row1, so includes (1,4) uncovered. So not allowed. Could we have tile covering left region cell (4,1) and right region cell (3,4)? Rectangle rows 3..4, columns 1..4 includes (3,3) uncovered (since uncovered at (3,3)). So not allowed. Could we have tile covering left region cell (3,2) and right region cell (2,2)? Wait (2,2) is right region? Row2 right region columns 2..4, so (2,2) is right region. Rectangle rows 2..3, columns 2..2 (vertical line column 2). That includes (2,2) and (3,2). (3,2) is left region cell. Does it include uncovered squares? Uncovered squares are (1,4), (2,1), (3,3), (4,2). The rectangle includes (4,2)? No, rows 2..3 only. So does it include (2,1) uncovered? No, column 2. Does it include (3,3) uncovered? No, column 2. So rectangle covering (2,2) and (3,2) does not include any uncovered squares. But does it intersect both left and right region? Yes: (3,2) is left region (since row3 left region columns 1..2, includes column 2). (2,2) is right region (since row2 right region columns 2..4). So this tile intersects both left and right region, but does not contain any uncovered squares. So Lemma 3 as stated is false! Let's verify the definitions: Left region L = {(i,j) | j < f(i)}. Right region R = {(i,j) | j > f(i)}. For row3, f(3)=3, left region includes columns 1,2. So (3,2) is left region. For row2, f(2)=1, right region includes columns >1, i.e., columns 2,3,4. So (2,2) is right region. So tile covering rows 2..3, columns 2..2 (a vertical line at column 2) includes (2,2) and (3,2). It does not include any uncovered squares (uncovered squares are at (1,4), (2,1), (3,3), (4,2)). Indeed, (4,2) is uncovered but row4 is not covered by tile. So tile does not intersect both regions? It does intersect both L and R: (3,2) in L, (2,2) in R. So Lemma 3 is false: there exists a tile intersecting both L and R without covering an uncovered square. However, the solution's Lemma 3 says \"A rectangle that contains a square of L and a square of R would have to contain, in some row it covers, a column occupied by an uncovered square, which is forbidden.\" In our example, tile covers rows 2 and 3. In row2,