w i, left region is 1..i-1; for row i+1, left region is 1..i; for row i+2, left region is 1..i+1; etc. So rectangle columns 1..i-1 would be a subset of left region for each row r >= i, but for row i+1, left region includes column i, which is not covered by rectangle. That's okay; rectangle does not need to cover all left region squares; we can cover them with other rectangles. So we could combine left parts of multiple rows into a single rectangle that covers columns 1..i-1 for rows i..j. That rectangle would avoid holes because holes are at columns r for each row r, which are >= i. So rectangle does not intersect any holes. So we can combine left parts of multiple rows into a single rectangle. Similarly, we can combine right parts of multiple rows into a rectangle covering columns j+1..n for rows i..j, provided holes are at columns r <= j. So we can reduce number of rectangles.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 may not be tight. Actually, we can tile the complement of the identity permutation with n rectangles: For each k from 1 to n-1, we can take rectangle covering rows 1..k, columns k+1..n (the region above diagonal for those rows). And also rectangle covering rows k+1..n, columns 1..k (region below diagonal). But those are not rectangles? Let's examine: The region above diagonal for rows 1..k includes squares where column > row, but row varies. If we take rectangle rows 1..k, columns k+1..n, that covers squares where column >= k+1 > row for all rows <= k. So it covers a subset of the above-diagonal region (the region where column > row). But does it cover all squares above diagonal? For row i <= k, columns > i include columns i+1..n. The rectangle columns k+1..n covers only columns >= k+1, so for row i < k, columns i+1..k are not covered. So we need additional rectangles to cover those. So not trivial.\n\nBetter approach: The complement of a permutation matrix is known as a \"rook board\" with one hole per row and column. The minimal number of rectangles needed t